WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.148
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.832
PRESENTER: Welcome to
the Kresge Auditorium.

00:00:14.832 --> 00:00:16.290
If you will please
take your seats,

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.870
we'll be starting momentarily.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:21.510
We request that you make sure
all cell phones and pagers are

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:22.710
turned off.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.930
And also, please note
that all audio and video

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:27.150
recording is prohibited.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.070
Please limit any
flash photography

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.795
to just the first one
minute of the presentation.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:38.280
Ladies and gentlemen, please
welcome MIT President Charles

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:38.790
Vest.

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:40.260
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:47.610
VEST: Thank you.

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:51.830
We want to welcome everyone
to today's discussion

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:56.690
and presentation by
Microsoft's Bill Gates.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:58.940
I think one of the
first things you observe

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:02.150
that is in the old days, the
academic used to come out

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:05.209
in a sweater to introduce
the corporate leader

00:01:05.209 --> 00:01:06.950
in the pinstripe suit.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.120
Today, we're going
to reverse that role.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.800
And I don't think that
will surprise anyone.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.310
And then again, Bill is
not your typical CEO.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.410
In fact, he is not a CEO.

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:22.160
As you may know, he is the
chief software architect

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:23.630
of Microsoft.

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:27.260
And he is indeed here today
to discuss with you some

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.040
of the grand challenges
facing computer science as we

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.020
move into the future.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:36.470
Bill's been a good friend of
MIT in many different ways

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:38.120
for many years.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.490
Starting next
September, many of you

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:43.100
are going to be
studying, and meeting,

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:46.640
and eating in the Gates Building
in the new Ray and Maria Stata

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.260
Center.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:52.550
Many of you already
are the recipients

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:54.440
of the work that
has been done here

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:58.790
at MIT under our
iCampus program done

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:02.270
and carried out in collaboration
with Microsoft Research.

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:06.290
That has really
reinvigorated and reinvented

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:10.370
much of teaching and
learning on the MIT campus.

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:14.000
But I also want to say in
particular that Bill Gates has

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.920
been a great enthusiast
for MIT's OpenCourseWare

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:22.580
initiative, something many
of us believe very deeply in.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:27.230
Because it is a way of taking
the knowledge and the pedagogy

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:30.470
that is generated
here at MIT and making

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:33.530
it accessible to people
in other institutions

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:37.670
all around the nation
and throughout the world.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:40.160
Bill is here today
to talk, as I said,

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.100
about the great
challenges facing

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:46.620
the future of computer science.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:50.360
But before he comes out,
I want to say something

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:52.410
quite personally.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:54.290
In addition to the work
that we're all here

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:57.620
to hear about today in computer
science and information

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:01.430
technology, Bill
and Melinda Gates

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:04.820
have done something
extraordinarily wonderful

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:08.870
for our planet in the work
that they are sponsoring,

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:12.860
to improve health and
to eradicate disease

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:17.240
throughout the developing world
and improve the lives of people

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:20.750
who live under conditions
that none of us in this room

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:22.630
can even imagine.

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:25.730
So please join me in
welcoming Bill Gates.

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:27.200
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:38.742 --> 00:03:39.450
GATES: Thank you.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:46.030
Well, it's great
to be here at MIT.

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:50.350
MIT has invented so many things
and played such a key role

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:52.960
in the advance of
computer science.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:54.640
I won't have time
to name them all.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.690
But Microsoft has been very
privileged to be associated

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:02.200
with MIT and in
helping collaborate

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.900
on a number of projects here.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.020
Microsoft is also-- has
some great employees

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:09.970
who came from MIT.

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:15.010
Most notably, people like
Gordon Bell, who's doing some--

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:18.940
still very creative after
making so many contributions.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:21.130
And also, Butler
Lampson, who's actually

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.770
back here teaching
courses as well as

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:27.510
continuing to help Microsoft.

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:29.400
The main thing I
want to present today

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.210
is how I see the next 10
years of computer science

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:36.060
as doing some really
amazing things,

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:40.020
solving some problems that have
been out there for many decades

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:43.980
and transforming most
things of interest,

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:47.340
transforming the way
people work in businesses,

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.010
the way they deal
with information

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:52.560
and meet and
communicate, transforming

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:56.640
the way that people at home find
information, the way that they

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.970
create things and connect
up with other people

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:04.230
and transform even the sciences,
bringing the methodologies--

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:07.140
machine learning, and
modeling, and rich data

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:10.350
mining into all of
the hard sciences

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:14.400
and allowing those scientists
to move to the next level.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.180
It's a paradox to me that
computer science today

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:20.880
is poised to do all
these amazing things

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:23.740
and yet, in some ways,
people's expectation

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:26.970
and even the excitement
level about computer science

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:32.280
is not as high today as it
was, say, five years ago,

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:35.160
when we were in the midst
of what we can now look back

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:38.280
and say was the internet bubble.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:41.449
Many of the challenges,
the problems,

00:05:41.449 --> 00:05:43.740
the things that were hard to
live on during that period

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:46.980
are exactly the kind of
things that a combination

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:51.240
of great academic work and great
commercial work coming together

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:52.830
has solved.

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:56.580
And we'll make sure that the
productivity advances that we

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:59.340
see in the economy will
be dramatically higher,

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:03.320
more than double what we
received in the 1990s.

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:07.770
And that's very profound
for innovation, employment,

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:11.610
many things on a global basis.

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:15.780
The computer industry
started by making

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:18.160
very big, expensive machines.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.300
And in fact, when I was young,
people thought of computers

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:25.110
as kind of these daunting
things in the back room that

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:27.300
would often print out
bills that would be wrong

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.310
and you could never
get corrected.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:32.220
People talked about taking
the punch card in your billing

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:34.680
envelope and putting
staples in it

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:38.730
or mutilating it to somehow
defeat the big machine.

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:44.070
And I was lucky enough
to be young at a time

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:45.520
when that changed.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:49.132
My friend Paul Allen saw the
very first microprocessor,

00:06:49.132 --> 00:06:51.030
the 8008--

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:55.065
not very capable, but
better than a PDP-8 was.

00:06:55.065 --> 00:06:56.461
And he challenged me.

00:06:56.461 --> 00:06:58.710
He said, hey, Bill, could
you write software for this?

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:00.900
Could you do a basic?

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:02.430
And that really got
us going, saying

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:05.200
that we wanted to be
on the ground floor,

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:07.890
building the kind of
software that computing

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:11.970
would need as it moved from
that back room onto the desktop,

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:14.130
as it became a tool
of empowerment,

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:17.380
a tool for creativity
and communications.

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:19.760
Now, it started in
a very humble way.

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:24.240
The kit computer that Paul
saw on the cover of Popular

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:28.350
Electronics magazine--
a freezing cold day

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:32.040
in Harvard Square and
brought back to me and said,

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:34.150
you got to drop out.

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:37.500
It's happening without us.

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:41.370
That machine was laughable.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:44.400
It at most had 8K of memory--

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:46.470
had no peripherals
to the software

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:49.370
we wrote-- would flash
lights and do funny things.

00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:51.620
It was a major discovery
that because of the noisiness

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:54.690
of the electronics in it,
that we could actually

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:56.130
put a radio nearby.

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:58.710
And if we did certain
construction patterns,

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:03.090
cause predictable music
to come out of that radio.

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:07.540
And it was kind of
limited what we could do,

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:08.730
but that was the beginning.

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:10.770
And the kind of
excitement around that,

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:14.000
thinking where could this go?

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:17.010
That got our industry
off to a great start.

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:19.350
There was a generation of
machines that came after that,

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:20.850
the so-called
[INAUDIBLE] machines--

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:25.500
TRS-80, Commodore 64,
Apple II, all of which

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:30.030
included Microsoft BASIC in
as their fundamental software.

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:32.789
It was the equivalent of
not only the language,

00:08:32.789 --> 00:08:33.870
but the operating system.

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:34.919
Everything.

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:37.230
And you could type
in basic statements

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:40.590
to do graphics, and games,
and business software.

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:43.320
And we even gotten disks
connected up to these things.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:46.050
We moved away from cassette
tapes and paper tapes

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:50.010
to these ridiculous
8-inch disks that

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:54.285
hardly held any information at
all, but constant improvement.

00:08:54.285 --> 00:08:59.820
A big milestone in 1981 was
the entry of IBM with the PC

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:01.140
into this business.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:04.110
And that had been a joint
project with Microsoft,

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:07.080
where we'd convinced
them to use the 8086,

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:09.300
created the DOS
operating system.

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:13.230
Very limited system, but very
appropriate for that machine.

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:16.930
And we kicked off an era
that was fairly different.

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:19.260
Because starting
with that machine,

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:21.750
we had a vision that we
wanted all the machines

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:22.650
to be compatible.

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:25.440
That is to use a
software layer to make it

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:27.990
so that whenever you
wrote an application,

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:30.540
it would run on machines
from IBM, or HP,

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:33.150
or Digital Equipment, or
any of the other computers

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:34.110
at the time.

00:09:34.110 --> 00:09:35.440
And that hadn't been the case.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:38.470
And in fact, that prevented
the virtuous cycle

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:40.580
that we wanted to have happen.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:42.090
It prevented it
from getting going.

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:46.740
And that cycle was that as
more people saw applications

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:49.452
that were meaningful to them,
they would buy machines.

00:09:49.452 --> 00:09:50.910
The more machines
that were bought,

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:52.701
the more volume of
components would happen,

00:09:52.701 --> 00:09:55.620
the lower the cost those
components would be,

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:59.130
and the more people would
invest in buying applications.

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:02.610
So it would make sense to really
build a software industry.

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:05.400
There essentially was
no software industry

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:06.480
before the PC.

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:08.820
There were about 20
different companies.

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:11.670
And the highest
award in the industry

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:13.980
was one that you got
for selling a thousand

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:15.765
copies of a piece of software.

00:10:15.765 --> 00:10:17.970
It was called the ICP Award.

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:20.370
And I filled out the
application after we'd sold

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:22.410
two million copies of BASIC.

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:23.340
I sent it in.

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:25.590
And they said, well, we'd
love to give you this award,

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:29.310
but there must be some
scientific notation error here.

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:30.630
You've got too many zeros.

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:32.225
And I said, no, I'm not kidding.

00:10:32.225 --> 00:10:35.100
We sold two million copies.

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:37.740
And they said, well, geez, how
come we haven't heard of you?

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:39.823
And we said, well, I don't
know where you've been,

00:10:39.823 --> 00:10:44.845
but we do have this very
high volume, low-price model.

00:10:44.845 --> 00:10:46.470
So we're not like on
the New York Stock

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:49.985
Exchange or anything, but we
have sold two million copies.

00:10:49.985 --> 00:10:52.260
And so personal computing
gained the momentum.

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:55.350
It gained in excitement.

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:57.900
One of the great
issues was the move

00:10:57.900 --> 00:11:01.220
from character mode interface
to graphics interface.

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:02.760
It may seem strange today.

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:04.260
Everyone takes that for granted.

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:07.230
But the cycles
required, the difficulty

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:09.622
of writing these programs
made a lot of people

00:11:09.622 --> 00:11:10.830
say, hey, we don't need this.

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:12.610
That's just frilly.

00:11:12.610 --> 00:11:15.010
Too many icons, too many fonts.

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:16.450
Let's stick to
the serious stuff.

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:19.360
We'd love these monospaced
characters up there

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:20.120
on the screen.

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:22.110
And there were about
six or seven years,

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:24.010
where taking some of
the pioneering work

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:27.190
from Xerox, Apple,
and Microsoft really

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:30.010
pushed forward with this
idea of graphics interface.

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:31.120
And that became--

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:35.160
Microsoft Windows was integrated
in the operating system.

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:37.570
And then we got to
the next frontier.

00:11:37.570 --> 00:11:41.050
That frontier was connecting
all the machines together.

00:11:41.050 --> 00:11:44.260
And year after year, we'd
always say, email is coming.

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:45.310
Connectivity is coming.

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:46.795
Online services are coming.

00:11:46.795 --> 00:11:49.480
And in fact, it didn't
happen and didn't happen.

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:52.990
And then all of a sudden,
coming out of the university

00:11:52.990 --> 00:11:56.710
environment, the standards
of the internet exploded,

00:11:56.710 --> 00:12:00.370
along with the decrease
in the cost of optic fiber

00:12:00.370 --> 00:12:02.710
and the increase of the
speed of the network.

00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:04.525
And a critical
mass was achieved.

00:12:04.525 --> 00:12:07.660
And that was the period
where things definitely

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:09.610
went a little crazy.

00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:11.290
But the gold rush
atmosphere actually

00:12:11.290 --> 00:12:13.870
accelerated the investment
people were making

00:12:13.870 --> 00:12:17.350
and raised the awareness that
people had of what a revolution

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:18.370
this was.

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:22.360
Now, some of the things that
are hard about e-commerce,

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:27.250
or workflow, or modeling, or
making sure these systems are

00:12:27.250 --> 00:12:30.250
ultra-reliable, ultra-secure--

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:33.130
some of those were
revealed as shortcomings

00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:36.610
that needed software
breakthroughs, needed software

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:37.480
advances.

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:42.011
And so as we look forward,
it's kind of a bias I have.

00:12:42.011 --> 00:12:44.260
But the thing that's really
going to make a difference

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:46.210
is software.

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:49.030
It is new generations
of software

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:51.730
that let us interact in
natural ways, that connect

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:54.130
these devices up in new ways.

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:59.740
And problems that I see being
solved in the near future.

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:01.080
Now, the hardware guys--

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:02.690
I have to give them credit.

00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:07.240
They've always provided a
more powerful platform for us

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:10.390
to exercise our software
creativity against.

00:13:10.390 --> 00:13:15.430
And that's why Paul Allen
and I could say back in 1975,

00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:18.100
personal computers
will be mainstream.

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:20.430
The slogan we had was a
computer on every desk

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:21.760
and in every home.

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:25.030
And in some small part
in some countries,

00:13:25.030 --> 00:13:27.490
we've come a long
ways towards that.

00:13:27.490 --> 00:13:29.650
It's not yet the
machine that we envisage

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:31.496
in terms of the ease
of use or breadth

00:13:31.496 --> 00:13:32.620
of things that can be done.

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:37.800
But it's certainly a good
rough draft that's on its way.

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:40.500
The hardware people have
given us Moore's law

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:42.630
that predicts doubling
in chip performance

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:44.760
every 18 to 24 months.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:47.910
That's held true
these last 25 years.

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:50.970
And something like the
next 10 to 15 years

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:53.950
it's very likely to
continue to hold true.

00:13:53.950 --> 00:13:56.170
Now, that increase
in transistors--

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:59.490
there are some very
interesting software techniques

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:01.050
related to
parallelization that are

00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:05.760
needed to take transistor count
and map it into performance.

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:07.590
It's not automatic
that just because you

00:14:07.590 --> 00:14:11.180
have twice as many transistors
that you get that performance.

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:13.630
And so finally,
some of these issues

00:14:13.630 --> 00:14:17.520
of automatic parallelization
and understanding the algorithms

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:19.140
that let you do that--

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:21.500
we're making progress on those.

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:24.720
The storage people
do an even better job

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:26.070
than the chip people.

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:30.000
Their doubling rate is
something like 12 to 18 months.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:31.170
And this is very important.

00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:34.260
Because when storage
was expensive,

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:38.250
the idea that you could deal
with photos, and videos,

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:41.730
and audio annotation, and
replicate information around,

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:44.970
so it'd be immediately available
even if the system's not

00:14:44.970 --> 00:14:46.830
connected up to the network.

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:49.140
That just wasn't possible.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:51.620
People didn't think
in those terms.

00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:53.910
And in fact, storage
is so available now.

00:14:53.910 --> 00:14:57.000
We have to be creative
in thinking about what

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:58.140
we're going to do with it.

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:01.590
We're getting lots
and lots of that.

00:15:01.590 --> 00:15:06.330
In fact, a good example
of how cheap storage is,

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:10.740
is that we have this device
that comes out this fall

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:12.660
called the Portable
Media Center.

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:17.860
It's a 4-gig disk,
beautiful LCD display.

00:15:17.860 --> 00:15:22.200
And you can just connect
this to a PC that's

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:26.430
recording TV shows, or has your
movies, or whatever is there.

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:30.960
And it automatically downloads
the movies, videos, photos

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:33.870
onto this device that you can,
of course, carry around and use

00:15:33.870 --> 00:15:35.340
any way, anywhere you want.

00:15:35.340 --> 00:15:39.731
And these devices will come out
fairly inexpensive in the $400

00:15:39.731 --> 00:15:40.230
range.

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:41.646
And the price will
just come down,

00:15:41.646 --> 00:15:46.950
and down, and down, because this
is the magic of that hardware

00:15:46.950 --> 00:15:47.940
innovation.

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:50.130
Eventually, we'll just
take it for granted

00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:52.050
that kids who want
to watch movies,

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:54.870
or people who want to watch
shows have this available.

00:15:54.870 --> 00:16:00.520
And so it won't just be portable
music players, but devices that

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:05.510
deal with the video as well.

00:16:05.510 --> 00:16:07.580
The screen is
another place where

00:16:07.580 --> 00:16:09.500
innovation is critical for us.

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:10.970
If we think about
how we can move

00:16:10.970 --> 00:16:14.240
reading from paper to
the screen so that we

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:18.920
get the rich searching,
updating, annotating, sharing

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:21.440
that the digital
world allows, that

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:24.170
requires screens with
very high resolution.

00:16:24.170 --> 00:16:27.590
It requires screens that we're
comfortable holding in our lap

00:16:27.590 --> 00:16:30.030
and just sitting there paging
through the information.

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:33.500
It requires a thin-like
device, long battery life.

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:35.210
Big challenges.

00:16:35.210 --> 00:16:38.180
But certainly what's
gone on with LCDs

00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:40.880
and other screen technologies
says that in the future,

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:45.500
we can assume a 30-inch
LCD on a knowledge worker's

00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:48.710
desktop or three
22-inch displays, which

00:16:48.710 --> 00:16:51.410
is the configuration
I'm using right now

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:53.750
and lets me work
with information

00:16:53.750 --> 00:16:55.220
in a much better way.

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:58.340
In the same way that a newspaper
gives you a big, wide field

00:16:58.340 --> 00:17:01.839
of vision, this does that.

00:17:01.839 --> 00:17:03.380
And there are
certainly some advances

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:06.140
in Windows management and
use of the screen area,

00:17:06.140 --> 00:17:08.950
that it has to take place as
we get to extremely high DPI

00:17:08.950 --> 00:17:12.560
in big screen areas
that come with this.

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:17.030
And we believe that reading will
move onto the digital platform,

00:17:17.030 --> 00:17:19.849
that the superiority of the
cost structure, all these things

00:17:19.849 --> 00:17:25.609
argue for that as we get
devices that are based

00:17:25.609 --> 00:17:27.619
on this new screen technology.

00:17:27.619 --> 00:17:31.580
A nice milestone in that is
the arrival of the tablet PC.

00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:33.530
That got kicked off
about a year ago.

00:17:33.530 --> 00:17:36.260
It's based on the
miniaturization hardware,

00:17:36.260 --> 00:17:38.380
the ink software.

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:40.550
Ink recognition
technology that we've

00:17:40.550 --> 00:17:42.575
been working on
for over 10 years

00:17:42.575 --> 00:17:46.036
is now bootstrapping in terms
of the quality of the hardware,

00:17:46.036 --> 00:17:48.410
learning from that software,
doing that better and better

00:17:48.410 --> 00:17:49.975
and making that mainstream.

00:17:49.975 --> 00:17:54.920
And so all the portable devices
will become tablet devices.

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:56.630
And they really will
be like tablets,

00:17:56.630 --> 00:18:01.100
which they're a little bit
heavier than a tablet today.

00:18:01.100 --> 00:18:06.080
The graphics processors--
there the improvements are you

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:07.760
can get higher
transistor counts there

00:18:07.760 --> 00:18:11.550
because the number of
duplicated components.

00:18:11.550 --> 00:18:15.380
And actually, if we look to
the future of CPU architecture,

00:18:15.380 --> 00:18:17.240
we can see that more
predicted by what's

00:18:17.240 --> 00:18:18.920
happening on the GPU level.

00:18:18.920 --> 00:18:22.220
Because they've thought
about parallelization.

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:24.130
Now, they do it in a
domain specific way

00:18:24.130 --> 00:18:25.130
that we need to open up.

00:18:25.130 --> 00:18:27.470
But it's really the
blending of those

00:18:27.470 --> 00:18:31.160
that is the next stage there.

00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:33.740
A big element, of
course, is wireless.

00:18:33.740 --> 00:18:36.320
As we get things like
ultra-wideband wireless, which

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:38.630
is hundreds of
megabits, the idea

00:18:38.630 --> 00:18:41.030
that you connect a
computer to the screen

00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:42.410
will become obsolete.

00:18:42.410 --> 00:18:45.020
The computer will find
the screen that's nearby

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:46.590
and take advantage of it.

00:18:46.590 --> 00:18:48.560
The idea that the
computer and the storage

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:50.610
have to be associated
with each other--

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:51.770
there's no reason for that.

00:18:51.770 --> 00:18:53.270
You can carry your
storage with you.

00:18:53.270 --> 00:18:56.180
And whatever PC is around
in a very secure way,

00:18:56.180 --> 00:18:58.912
your storage can be
made available to it.

00:18:58.912 --> 00:19:01.370
And you can be given guarantees
that that information isn't

00:19:01.370 --> 00:19:05.050
left on that machine after
you log out from that machine.

00:19:05.050 --> 00:19:08.780
So we'll see the desegregation
of the PC that way.

00:19:08.780 --> 00:19:11.630
We'll see the arrival
of rich new peripherals.

00:19:11.630 --> 00:19:15.680
Digital cameras are now the
most popular way to take photos.

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:17.930
And that's happening in
the motion video space.

00:19:17.930 --> 00:19:20.660
Well, those devices will
have ultra-wideband.

00:19:20.660 --> 00:19:23.510
So they'll mark not only
the time, but the location

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:27.340
of that information and deliver
that to your storage system.

00:19:27.340 --> 00:19:29.090
Your storage system
would be a combination

00:19:29.090 --> 00:19:33.890
of data stored in the cloud
in sort of a far-sight, ocean

00:19:33.890 --> 00:19:36.140
stored-type way, where you
don't have to worry about

00:19:36.140 --> 00:19:38.090
whether you've backed it up.

00:19:38.090 --> 00:19:40.520
Because there's many
copies, but that

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:43.970
are stored in encrypted
ways that mean only

00:19:43.970 --> 00:19:46.022
you have control of
that information.

00:19:46.022 --> 00:19:47.480
Or you'll have the
storage that you

00:19:47.480 --> 00:19:49.820
carry with you physically
that'll give you

00:19:49.820 --> 00:19:50.840
total control over it.

00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:52.790
And making those two
things work well together

00:19:52.790 --> 00:19:54.350
is very important.

00:19:54.350 --> 00:19:57.020
Now, in the area of wireless,
one of the top challenges

00:19:57.020 --> 00:19:59.360
has been the cost of broadband.

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:02.930
When we think about what's
expensive, getting a PC,

00:20:02.930 --> 00:20:04.390
say, into rural India.

00:20:04.390 --> 00:20:06.750
And the hardware is 3 or $400.

00:20:06.750 --> 00:20:09.440
The software is less than $50.

00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:12.890
It's that broadband
cost, the monthly cost

00:20:12.890 --> 00:20:15.770
of paying again and again and
getting that infrastructure

00:20:15.770 --> 00:20:18.590
out there that's really
the prohibited factor.

00:20:18.590 --> 00:20:23.630
And we believe through some
software techniques around mesh

00:20:23.630 --> 00:20:27.050
networks and some advances
in the wireless hardware,

00:20:27.050 --> 00:20:31.400
particularly the WiMAX-type
approaches bringing

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:33.350
in not just
omnidirectional approaches,

00:20:33.350 --> 00:20:35.690
but directional antenna
as well, that we

00:20:35.690 --> 00:20:39.230
will get the kind of
connectivity that can make sure

00:20:39.230 --> 00:20:43.990
that connecting everyone on the
planet becomes very feasible.

00:20:43.990 --> 00:20:46.750
So you'll have a
range of devices--

00:20:46.750 --> 00:20:52.480
wall-sized screen devices,
desktop, tablet, pocket size.

00:20:52.480 --> 00:20:55.900
We even believe in a
wrist-sized device.

00:20:55.900 --> 00:21:00.190
In fact, I'm wearing my SPOT
watch, which just came out

00:21:00.190 --> 00:21:02.370
in the last month.

00:21:02.370 --> 00:21:05.320
And this-- I don't
know if you've seen it.

00:21:05.320 --> 00:21:09.190
It lets you see
sports activities,

00:21:09.190 --> 00:21:11.410
stock prices, your schedule.

00:21:11.410 --> 00:21:13.330
You get messages on it.

00:21:13.330 --> 00:21:15.460
A lot of different
things that are being

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:17.350
transmitted to this watch.

00:21:17.350 --> 00:21:20.950
It was actually when I was
at MIT over 10 years ago

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:26.320
that I first saw a demonstration
of FM sideband data networking.

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:30.160
And this watch is
based on that approach.

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:31.900
Of course, the
modulation techniques

00:21:31.900 --> 00:21:34.420
are several generations later.

00:21:34.420 --> 00:21:36.940
There's a microprocessor
in here that we

00:21:36.940 --> 00:21:40.930
paid National Semiconductor to
create that's based on the ARM

00:21:40.930 --> 00:21:42.250
architecture.

00:21:42.250 --> 00:21:44.920
This microprocessor
just on my wrist

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:49.870
has 10 times the power
of the original IBM PC.

00:21:49.870 --> 00:21:54.100
It's got 10 times the memory
of the original IBM PC.

00:21:54.100 --> 00:21:56.320
Now, this thing is powerful.

00:21:56.320 --> 00:21:59.410
And of course, the
battery life is

00:21:59.410 --> 00:22:03.657
on the order of many, many
days, because these things

00:22:03.657 --> 00:22:04.240
are low power.

00:22:04.240 --> 00:22:06.990
We can download arbitrary
programs to this device.

00:22:06.990 --> 00:22:10.180
So as we get new ideas
about sports presentation,

00:22:10.180 --> 00:22:12.250
information
presentation, as people

00:22:12.250 --> 00:22:13.990
have neat things
they want to do,

00:22:13.990 --> 00:22:17.330
it can come down in
an automatic way.

00:22:17.330 --> 00:22:19.480
Today the watch is
in receive-only mode.

00:22:19.480 --> 00:22:21.310
But we actually
have the capability

00:22:21.310 --> 00:22:24.150
to send data as well
in a local area.

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:26.530
And so you can find people
with common interest.

00:22:26.530 --> 00:22:30.970
A lot of applications that the
glancible information platform

00:22:30.970 --> 00:22:33.670
will be particularly
appropriate for,

00:22:33.670 --> 00:22:35.635
working with the other devices.

00:22:35.635 --> 00:22:37.510
Now, we have to think
in terms of scenarios--

00:22:37.510 --> 00:22:40.640
the photo scenario that you
use all the different devices,

00:22:40.640 --> 00:22:44.710
the scheduling scenario
using all those devices.

00:22:44.710 --> 00:22:47.350
And it's way too
far today to get

00:22:47.350 --> 00:22:50.100
those things to work together.

00:22:50.100 --> 00:22:54.320
One of the big places that
software advances will

00:22:54.320 --> 00:22:57.770
change things is the
way business is done.

00:22:57.770 --> 00:23:01.730
The information visibility
that a typical information

00:23:01.730 --> 00:23:05.090
worker has is extremely low.

00:23:05.090 --> 00:23:07.880
They're used to it in a way,
so they don't know to complain.

00:23:07.880 --> 00:23:11.630
But their ability-- say somebody
gives them a sales printout.

00:23:11.630 --> 00:23:13.280
They look at these numbers.

00:23:13.280 --> 00:23:15.950
And they must think, wow,
that one's really big.

00:23:15.950 --> 00:23:16.969
Wow, what did we do?

00:23:16.969 --> 00:23:18.260
Now, that one is kind of small.

00:23:18.260 --> 00:23:19.880
Geez, are we in trouble?

00:23:19.880 --> 00:23:22.130
Well, their ability to
just dive into that data

00:23:22.130 --> 00:23:24.320
and see it by time
period, product,

00:23:24.320 --> 00:23:26.570
cost structure--
they don't have it.

00:23:26.570 --> 00:23:27.920
It's not there.

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:30.170
The schematization
and model approach

00:23:30.170 --> 00:23:32.450
to bring that down
to every employee

00:23:32.450 --> 00:23:35.910
to just naturally expect that
they can see those things

00:23:35.910 --> 00:23:38.090
and understand those
things-- that's not there.

00:23:38.090 --> 00:23:40.670
The world of
business intelligence

00:23:40.670 --> 00:23:42.250
hasn't delivered on that.

00:23:42.250 --> 00:23:45.440
The XMLFoundation that's
advanced so fantastically

00:23:45.440 --> 00:23:49.070
over these last six years is the
foundation to make that happen,

00:23:49.070 --> 00:23:51.560
to build XML into
the spreadsheet,

00:23:51.560 --> 00:23:54.690
to build a knowledge
of business processes,

00:23:54.690 --> 00:23:59.090
so you can visually see, what's
the state of this activity?

00:23:59.090 --> 00:24:02.600
Businesses today do all
these custom modifications

00:24:02.600 --> 00:24:05.170
to the applications
programs they run--

00:24:05.170 --> 00:24:07.190
the enterprise applications.

00:24:07.190 --> 00:24:09.320
And it's very strange.

00:24:09.320 --> 00:24:11.630
Because the differences
between those businesses--

00:24:11.630 --> 00:24:15.680
you ought to be able to express
it in some other way than code.

00:24:15.680 --> 00:24:17.750
Code is complex.

00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:19.379
When people update
the applications,

00:24:19.379 --> 00:24:21.170
you don't know how to
combine that new code

00:24:21.170 --> 00:24:24.050
with the other code,
because it's not orthogonal.

00:24:24.050 --> 00:24:26.330
There shouldn't be
code in that process.

00:24:26.330 --> 00:24:29.360
There should just be
visual business processes

00:24:29.360 --> 00:24:32.990
that you're connecting up to and
explaining how this business is

00:24:32.990 --> 00:24:35.017
different than this business.

00:24:35.017 --> 00:24:36.350
How does the order process work?

00:24:36.350 --> 00:24:37.891
How does the collection
process work?

00:24:37.891 --> 00:24:39.930
How does the analysis
process work?

00:24:39.930 --> 00:24:42.620
And that's kind of the thing
we're really on the verge of.

00:24:42.620 --> 00:24:45.290
Because XML gets our
semantic level up and lets

00:24:45.290 --> 00:24:49.915
us finally address making this
information really available.

00:24:49.915 --> 00:24:52.010
If we looked at
meetings, meetings

00:24:52.010 --> 00:24:55.230
are a source of a
lot of inefficiency.

00:24:55.230 --> 00:24:57.840
Any information
worker will tell you

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:00.641
things that they didn't need
to be there for, things they--

00:25:00.641 --> 00:25:03.140
meetings they had to fly into
that they would have preferred

00:25:03.140 --> 00:25:04.910
to be able to do at a distance.

00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:06.560
Things that didn't
get followed up on.

00:25:06.560 --> 00:25:08.390
Things that somebody
who wasn't there.

00:25:08.390 --> 00:25:11.360
You wanted to explain to
them that you couldn't just

00:25:11.360 --> 00:25:14.750
link in and see the transcript
or see the video of what

00:25:14.750 --> 00:25:15.800
went on there.

00:25:15.800 --> 00:25:18.890
Well, storage is almost free.

00:25:18.890 --> 00:25:22.520
And cameras and software to
scan and understand this stuff

00:25:22.520 --> 00:25:23.930
will be almost free.

00:25:23.930 --> 00:25:27.200
And so we can take the meeting
and have that be something

00:25:27.200 --> 00:25:30.110
that we bring a lot
of efficiency to.

00:25:30.110 --> 00:25:33.960
If you make meetings in
general 10% more efficient,

00:25:33.960 --> 00:25:38.330
that's tens of billions of
dollars of X for productivity

00:25:38.330 --> 00:25:39.950
every year.

00:25:39.950 --> 00:25:41.760
And that can be used
just as cost savings.

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:43.820
It can be used to
make better decisions,

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:45.710
to drive quality into processes.

00:25:45.710 --> 00:25:48.290
And it will do every
one of those things.

00:25:48.290 --> 00:25:51.580
Even the basic process
of buying and selling

00:25:51.580 --> 00:25:55.400
hasn't been made as
efficient as it should be.

00:25:55.400 --> 00:25:58.700
Can you find all the sellers of
a particular type of product?

00:25:58.700 --> 00:26:01.010
Can you check their reputation?

00:26:01.010 --> 00:26:02.750
If you engage in
a transaction, see

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:05.510
the state of that transaction
in a very rich way?

00:26:05.510 --> 00:26:08.210
If your computer is
talking to their computer

00:26:08.210 --> 00:26:09.930
and their computer
is somehow malicious,

00:26:09.930 --> 00:26:13.099
are you protected from
that kind of behavior?

00:26:13.099 --> 00:26:15.140
If the software is talking
to the other software,

00:26:15.140 --> 00:26:17.270
what about the workers?

00:26:17.270 --> 00:26:19.610
Say that there's a
delivery that's defective.

00:26:19.610 --> 00:26:22.970
How do you coordinate
the negotiation on email

00:26:22.970 --> 00:26:25.940
in an ad hoc way with
these back end systems,

00:26:25.940 --> 00:26:28.920
so they can understand things
and check the state of things?

00:26:28.920 --> 00:26:31.130
These things are incredibly
inefficient today.

00:26:31.130 --> 00:26:33.740
So basic workflow
is not built in.

00:26:33.740 --> 00:26:35.600
E-commerce has not happened.

00:26:35.600 --> 00:26:39.050
E-commerce only really
happens where every seller

00:26:39.050 --> 00:26:40.190
can find every buyer.

00:26:40.190 --> 00:26:43.820
Every buyer can find every
seller independent of location

00:26:43.820 --> 00:26:46.220
or previous knowledge
of each other.

00:26:46.220 --> 00:26:52.070
And that rich transaction is
done in a pure digital way.

00:26:52.070 --> 00:26:54.750
In communications,
what we've got today

00:26:54.750 --> 00:26:57.570
is kind of a hodgepodge
of different things.

00:26:57.570 --> 00:26:59.310
The latest thing is blogging.

00:26:59.310 --> 00:27:00.990
That comes after
instant messaging,

00:27:00.990 --> 00:27:02.730
which comes after email.

00:27:02.730 --> 00:27:05.250
You've got your wireless
phone, your wired phone.

00:27:05.250 --> 00:27:06.630
Lots of times
you're interrupted.

00:27:06.630 --> 00:27:08.340
The phone rings when
you don't want it.

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:11.590
Things come into your
inbox you don't want.

00:27:11.590 --> 00:27:14.040
And your time is
a scarce resource.

00:27:14.040 --> 00:27:17.430
And so these activities
are wasting your time,

00:27:17.430 --> 00:27:19.170
causing a lack of productivity.

00:27:19.170 --> 00:27:21.330
Even in some cases,
you have enough spam

00:27:21.330 --> 00:27:24.300
that you filter out
or don't have time

00:27:24.300 --> 00:27:28.260
to read mail that would
have been of value.

00:27:28.260 --> 00:27:30.270
Now, for me, spam--

00:27:30.270 --> 00:27:31.870
it's this awful thing.

00:27:31.870 --> 00:27:34.890
But sometimes when I look
at these spams I get,

00:27:34.890 --> 00:27:38.460
I have to just step back
and laugh about them.

00:27:38.460 --> 00:27:41.572
I've got a few examples here.

00:27:41.572 --> 00:27:42.780
This is one of my first ones.

00:27:42.780 --> 00:27:44.181
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:44.181 --> 00:27:45.582
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:48.390 --> 00:27:50.340
And it's clear once
I get out of debt,

00:27:50.340 --> 00:27:53.480
I'm going to be meeting
a lot of nice people

00:27:53.480 --> 00:27:55.860
who are going to
be friendly to me.

00:27:55.860 --> 00:28:00.220
The next one looked like
it might be more targeted.

00:28:00.220 --> 00:28:04.470
And this is not
one that any of you

00:28:04.470 --> 00:28:09.930
need worry about, since I
hope you won't drop out.

00:28:09.930 --> 00:28:11.610
And finally, there
was one that really

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:14.330
related to a serious
cost problem I've got.

00:28:20.540 --> 00:28:24.200
The shareholders really want
me to dig into this one,

00:28:24.200 --> 00:28:26.480
understand what's
going on there.

00:28:26.480 --> 00:28:28.580
So it's a serious problem.

00:28:28.580 --> 00:28:31.510
But it's amazing the
things that are out there.

00:28:31.510 --> 00:28:35.030
Letting people send billions
of pieces of mail very,

00:28:35.030 --> 00:28:39.410
very cheaply devalues the time
of the person on the other end.

00:28:39.410 --> 00:28:42.220
And this is a very
solvable problem.

00:28:42.220 --> 00:28:44.500
We need mail that
comes from people

00:28:44.500 --> 00:28:48.070
we communicate with regularly
to be authenticatable.

00:28:48.070 --> 00:28:51.730
And we announce on Tuesday a way
of doing that, leveraging off

00:28:51.730 --> 00:28:56.690
the DNS that we think
can be applied and used

00:28:56.690 --> 00:29:00.140
as a standard,
literally within months.

00:29:00.140 --> 00:29:02.470
Mail that comes in
from a stranger--

00:29:02.470 --> 00:29:05.740
some type of proof is necessary.

00:29:05.740 --> 00:29:09.230
If the filter thinks
that looks like spam,

00:29:09.230 --> 00:29:11.980
then you need some type
of proof to distinguish it

00:29:11.980 --> 00:29:13.450
from the other email.

00:29:13.450 --> 00:29:16.810
And there's several forms
of proof that will be used,

00:29:16.810 --> 00:29:18.470
and they all work in parallel.

00:29:18.470 --> 00:29:21.360
Any one of them is kind
of an "or" condition.

00:29:21.360 --> 00:29:24.280
Proof that's
computational, where

00:29:24.280 --> 00:29:25.870
you solve one of
these problems that's

00:29:25.870 --> 00:29:29.330
asymmetric in the opposite way
that cryptographic functions

00:29:29.330 --> 00:29:29.830
are.

00:29:29.830 --> 00:29:33.610
That is it's asymmetric in the
sense that checking the answer

00:29:33.610 --> 00:29:38.750
is easy, but actually doing
the computation is hard.

00:29:38.750 --> 00:29:41.500
And so for somebody sending
a modest amount of mail,

00:29:41.500 --> 00:29:42.875
it'll just happen in background.

00:29:42.875 --> 00:29:43.750
They won't notice it.

00:29:43.750 --> 00:29:46.090
But if you were sending
millions of emails,

00:29:46.090 --> 00:29:49.870
it would be a significant
computation cost to do that.

00:29:49.870 --> 00:29:51.580
So you screen out.

00:29:51.580 --> 00:29:54.190
Human interactive proof where
you bounce back and make

00:29:54.190 --> 00:29:57.680
somebody solve-- it's something
that software alone can't do--

00:29:57.680 --> 00:29:58.870
is another approach.

00:29:58.870 --> 00:30:01.780
Or if there's a connection
into a payment system making

00:30:01.780 --> 00:30:04.190
somebody put a little
bit of money at risk,

00:30:04.190 --> 00:30:06.190
not that gets charged to
them-- and this is only

00:30:06.190 --> 00:30:09.110
email from strangers,
but that is at risk.

00:30:09.110 --> 00:30:11.726
So that if it really is junk,
the person who receives it

00:30:11.726 --> 00:30:14.350
and spends time reading it-- at
least they get the benefit that

00:30:14.350 --> 00:30:17.240
whatever the threshold
they set for their time,

00:30:17.240 --> 00:30:19.840
their inbox rate--

00:30:19.840 --> 00:30:22.930
that gets credited to them.

00:30:22.930 --> 00:30:24.940
But if it's their long
lost brother or somebody

00:30:24.940 --> 00:30:27.460
saying their house is
on fire, hopefully they

00:30:27.460 --> 00:30:28.990
won't debit that.

00:30:28.990 --> 00:30:31.420
They'll say, okay,
that was just at risk.

00:30:31.420 --> 00:30:35.940
And I'm glad that person
can't connect it up to me.

00:30:35.940 --> 00:30:40.240
In the home environment when we
think about media and memories,

00:30:40.240 --> 00:30:41.680
there's so much
that can be done.

00:30:41.680 --> 00:30:44.500
And yet, we're going to have
to deal with a lot of volume--

00:30:44.500 --> 00:30:46.540
all the music you
like, all the movies

00:30:46.540 --> 00:30:50.210
that you're interested in, all
those photos that you take.

00:30:50.210 --> 00:30:51.140
It's kind of amazing.

00:30:51.140 --> 00:30:54.700
We have a researcher at
Microsoft Research who wears

00:30:54.700 --> 00:30:56.800
this-- as they go
around in the day--

00:30:56.800 --> 00:30:58.810
something that's just a camera.

00:30:58.810 --> 00:31:01.300
And it notices when
there's a big scene change,

00:31:01.300 --> 00:31:04.960
or when there's people laughing,
or anything loud or something.

00:31:04.960 --> 00:31:06.552
And it takes photos.

00:31:06.552 --> 00:31:08.260
So at the end of the
day, that researcher

00:31:08.260 --> 00:31:10.840
has over 100 photos
that might be

00:31:10.840 --> 00:31:13.810
interesting to
put in her journal

00:31:13.810 --> 00:31:17.170
and save and even annotate
with some voice or something.

00:31:17.170 --> 00:31:20.290
But software has
got to help select

00:31:20.290 --> 00:31:22.100
which one of those
things are interesting

00:31:22.100 --> 00:31:25.630
and to navigate
amongst those things.

00:31:25.630 --> 00:31:28.840
And so there-- a lot that
has to be done on that.

00:31:28.840 --> 00:31:31.360
We want to put users
in control in the home.

00:31:31.360 --> 00:31:34.630
This idea of watching TV
shows only on a schedule--

00:31:34.630 --> 00:31:37.340
slowly but surely
that's going away.

00:31:37.340 --> 00:31:38.782
People use that.

00:31:38.782 --> 00:31:40.990
Whether it's built into what
we call the Media Center

00:31:40.990 --> 00:31:44.830
PC, or a TiVo, or the
satellite receiver--

00:31:44.830 --> 00:31:46.280
get very addicted to it.

00:31:46.280 --> 00:31:48.790
It's kind of like email
where it's not perfect,

00:31:48.790 --> 00:31:50.720
but you don't want
to give it up.

00:31:50.720 --> 00:31:53.690
You just want it to get better.

00:31:53.690 --> 00:31:55.720
And so as we think
about that, we

00:31:55.720 --> 00:31:59.380
think about what
kind of interfaces

00:31:59.380 --> 00:32:01.580
would deliver on that.

00:32:01.580 --> 00:32:04.510
And in fact, I've got just real
quickly a couple prototypes

00:32:04.510 --> 00:32:05.600
from Microsoft Research.

00:32:05.600 --> 00:32:08.950
I wanted to give
just a sense of this.

00:32:08.950 --> 00:32:13.540
In fact, these were both
done by an MIT graduate.

00:32:13.540 --> 00:32:16.160
The first one here is
pretty straightforward.

00:32:16.160 --> 00:32:17.830
Let's say you're
looking at movies.

00:32:17.830 --> 00:32:20.140
You're looking at
Blade Runner here.

00:32:20.140 --> 00:32:22.572
And what it shows is-- okay,
the director is Ridley Scott.

00:32:22.572 --> 00:32:25.030
Well, then I can go over here
and see other movies directed

00:32:25.030 --> 00:32:26.230
by Ridley Scott.

00:32:26.230 --> 00:32:28.570
And I can just
select one of those--

00:32:28.570 --> 00:32:29.342
Alien.

00:32:29.342 --> 00:32:30.550
That's brought to the center.

00:32:30.550 --> 00:32:32.110
And of course,
then all the things

00:32:32.110 --> 00:32:33.370
related to that come out.

00:32:33.370 --> 00:32:34.900
And I can see--
okay, these actors

00:32:34.900 --> 00:32:36.730
and see the different
things they were in

00:32:36.730 --> 00:32:39.250
and see if one of those
might be interesting.

00:32:39.250 --> 00:32:41.650
And just pivot
through these sets

00:32:41.650 --> 00:32:44.140
of movies in a
simple, visual way.

00:32:44.140 --> 00:32:46.330
Of course, this will be
annotated with the reviews

00:32:46.330 --> 00:32:49.330
that you trust,
comments from friends.

00:32:49.330 --> 00:32:51.500
If you've seen the movie,
what you thought about it.

00:32:51.500 --> 00:32:56.766
And so very navigable to get
around the movies of interest.

00:32:56.766 --> 00:32:59.790
The other one I wanted to
show has to do with photos.

00:32:59.790 --> 00:33:04.480
And photos-- we're dealing
with lots and lots of photos.

00:33:04.480 --> 00:33:08.756
Literally, if you take your
lifetime, tens of thousands

00:33:08.756 --> 00:33:10.755
of photos that you and
your friends are sharing,

00:33:10.755 --> 00:33:15.120
and you'd like to be able to
get back to in a rich way.

00:33:15.120 --> 00:33:16.740
And so here we see
them as miniatures.

00:33:16.740 --> 00:33:18.720
I can just hover
over these things.

00:33:18.720 --> 00:33:19.830
Lots of photos.

00:33:19.830 --> 00:33:23.400
We've even mixed in
video clips as well.

00:33:23.400 --> 00:33:27.360
Here's Gordon Bell at
the Computer Museum.

00:33:27.360 --> 00:33:29.940
Because we don't think the
boundary between stills

00:33:29.940 --> 00:33:31.110
and motion will hold up.

00:33:31.110 --> 00:33:33.600
In fact, these audio
comments that we

00:33:33.600 --> 00:33:37.350
call photo stories bring a
lot more emotional connection

00:33:37.350 --> 00:33:38.870
to that experience.

00:33:38.870 --> 00:33:41.880
And so it's just like this.

00:33:41.880 --> 00:33:43.710
It's hard to find
exactly what you want.

00:33:43.710 --> 00:33:45.980
And so people will take
these things with keywords.

00:33:45.980 --> 00:33:48.660
Here is things that
relate to Thanksgiving.

00:33:48.660 --> 00:33:50.670
We can do software analysis.

00:33:50.670 --> 00:33:53.790
And so if we want the
photos with faces,

00:33:53.790 --> 00:33:54.860
we just select those.

00:33:54.860 --> 00:33:56.490
If we want the photos
that are indoors,

00:33:56.490 --> 00:33:58.365
the software can select those.

00:33:58.365 --> 00:34:01.740
If we want the outdoor
photos, we can select those.

00:34:01.740 --> 00:34:04.600
If we want to see
photos that are similar,

00:34:04.600 --> 00:34:08.940
let's select this bridge
photo and say, okay,

00:34:08.940 --> 00:34:11.639
I can relax the constraint and
say, what's similar to that?

00:34:11.639 --> 00:34:13.350
Okay, that's a lot like it.

00:34:13.350 --> 00:34:14.820
That's a little
bit more like it.

00:34:14.820 --> 00:34:18.409
And I can select groups
of things to be used.

00:34:18.409 --> 00:34:20.840
This software automatically--
when it brought the photos

00:34:20.840 --> 00:34:24.920
in, helped orient the photo
by being able to recognize

00:34:24.920 --> 00:34:28.020
the cases where
things were kind of--

00:34:28.020 --> 00:34:31.780
at least of the software looked
like they might be misoriented.

00:34:31.780 --> 00:34:34.469
We can also start
to use a 3D way

00:34:34.469 --> 00:34:36.969
of looking at these things
to group these things.

00:34:36.969 --> 00:34:38.190
And what that means--

00:34:38.190 --> 00:34:39.449
now, this is by timeline.

00:34:39.449 --> 00:34:41.670
So I can select a
set and take these.

00:34:41.670 --> 00:34:45.630
I can change the timeline,
get to finer groups

00:34:45.630 --> 00:34:48.480
in terms of when they were
taken or where they were taken.

00:34:48.480 --> 00:34:50.880
And this makes it very easy
to just step through these,

00:34:50.880 --> 00:34:55.170
but also deal with groups that
I want to organize and take

00:34:55.170 --> 00:34:56.254
in different ways.

00:34:56.254 --> 00:34:58.320
So in a general sense,
we can say, well,

00:34:58.320 --> 00:35:00.000
that's just a database.

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:03.570
But we need much better ways
of interacting with a database

00:35:03.570 --> 00:35:06.300
than just the common
query processor.

00:35:06.300 --> 00:35:08.880
People won't be
writing SQL statements

00:35:08.880 --> 00:35:12.500
to navigate through
their photos.

00:35:12.500 --> 00:35:14.900
Now, this optimism I have
about computer science

00:35:14.900 --> 00:35:17.060
and its impact--

00:35:17.060 --> 00:35:22.580
a little bit the proof of
how serious we are about that

00:35:22.580 --> 00:35:27.770
is the R&amp;D spending that has
been increasing at Microsoft.

00:35:27.770 --> 00:35:30.520
Today it's $6.8 billion a year.

00:35:30.520 --> 00:35:33.500
It's kind of an intimidating
number, at least to me,

00:35:33.500 --> 00:35:36.380
since 10 years from now people
will say to me whether that

00:35:36.380 --> 00:35:38.890
was wise or not.

00:35:38.890 --> 00:35:41.240
But I'm quite
confident that it is.

00:35:41.240 --> 00:35:45.470
That's the largest technology
R&amp;D budget that there is.

00:35:45.470 --> 00:35:48.640
IBM is about 20% less than that.

00:35:48.640 --> 00:35:51.350
But of course, not all
folks own software.

00:35:51.350 --> 00:35:53.300
And then other
commercial entities--

00:35:53.300 --> 00:35:55.940
you'd have a big drop down,
particularly if you take

00:35:55.940 --> 00:35:59.215
the long-term component--

00:35:59.215 --> 00:36:01.625
the equivalent of
Microsoft Research.

00:36:01.625 --> 00:36:04.250
We actually do our research work
in three different locations--

00:36:04.250 --> 00:36:08.280
in Cambridge, and in our
headquarters, and in Beijing.

00:36:08.280 --> 00:36:11.040
We have smaller groups
in some other areas,

00:36:11.040 --> 00:36:14.210
but those are the primary areas.

00:36:14.210 --> 00:36:17.390
We've had, as I mentioned
earlier, a strong collaboration

00:36:17.390 --> 00:36:19.760
on a number of things with MIT.

00:36:19.760 --> 00:36:21.770
For example, the
iCampus project.

00:36:21.770 --> 00:36:23.420
We're a key partner in that.

00:36:23.420 --> 00:36:26.210
And I'm thrilled-- the things
that are coming out of that.

00:36:26.210 --> 00:36:29.630
Some involve learning
at a distance.

00:36:29.630 --> 00:36:32.190
Some involve the tablet PC.

00:36:32.190 --> 00:36:34.550
And the idea that we can make
learning better-- there's

00:36:34.550 --> 00:36:35.340
no doubt.

00:36:35.340 --> 00:36:38.800
And I think that's a
great planning project.

00:36:38.800 --> 00:36:40.510
Natural interfaces--
I'll throw in.

00:36:40.510 --> 00:36:42.530
And that's this
magic paper idea.

00:36:42.530 --> 00:36:45.340
That's a fantastic thing.

00:36:45.340 --> 00:36:48.770
And we've gotten involved
in things that think.

00:36:48.770 --> 00:36:50.840
We've got a lot of
people on the faculty

00:36:50.840 --> 00:36:52.700
here that are helping
drive our agenda.

00:36:52.700 --> 00:36:55.820
For example, Victor Zue, who's
on the technical advisory board

00:36:55.820 --> 00:36:58.220
for our group over in Beijing.

00:36:58.220 --> 00:37:01.970
So it's been a good,
strong relationship.

00:37:01.970 --> 00:37:05.780
And the progress being
made in the combination

00:37:05.780 --> 00:37:08.240
of academia and
commercial research labs

00:37:08.240 --> 00:37:10.250
is really fascinating.

00:37:10.250 --> 00:37:14.380
It's phenomenal and it's not
getting that much visibility.

00:37:14.380 --> 00:37:19.670
And yet, these advances are
extremely relevant to problems

00:37:19.670 --> 00:37:24.440
that we have-- the problems
that are of critical importance.

00:37:24.440 --> 00:37:26.230
Take for example, security.

00:37:26.230 --> 00:37:30.620
Of course, MIT has strong
programming on that.

00:37:30.620 --> 00:37:32.510
Of course, you've
got Professor Rivest,

00:37:32.510 --> 00:37:36.860
who just got the Turing Award,
which is a fantastic thing.

00:37:36.860 --> 00:37:39.020
Security is something
that if we're

00:37:39.020 --> 00:37:41.540
going to achieve the
potential, these systems--

00:37:41.540 --> 00:37:44.450
has to get a lot better.

00:37:44.450 --> 00:37:47.030
And that's a tough thing,
because code reliability

00:37:47.030 --> 00:37:50.280
gets into that, how you
configure up these systems.

00:37:50.280 --> 00:37:52.350
How you watch behavior
is part of that.

00:37:52.350 --> 00:37:54.440
So it's going to take
some breakthroughs.

00:37:54.440 --> 00:37:59.480
Over 25 years ago when
I was leaving Harvard,

00:37:59.480 --> 00:38:01.400
this idea of proving
program correctness

00:38:01.400 --> 00:38:03.230
was sort of in vogue.

00:38:03.230 --> 00:38:04.916
And unfortunately
for many years,

00:38:04.916 --> 00:38:06.290
although there
was some progress,

00:38:06.290 --> 00:38:09.530
the scale of the program
that could be played against

00:38:09.530 --> 00:38:13.060
didn't get very large--
hundreds of lines of code.

00:38:13.060 --> 00:38:15.860
And now, working
with universities,

00:38:15.860 --> 00:38:19.130
people in Microsoft Research
are taking literally things that

00:38:19.130 --> 00:38:21.170
are a million lines
of code and being

00:38:21.170 --> 00:38:23.600
able to go in and prove
very important things

00:38:23.600 --> 00:38:24.740
about those programs.

00:38:24.740 --> 00:38:26.240
Or if they can't
prove them, they're

00:38:26.240 --> 00:38:28.730
able to show the
counterexample that says,

00:38:28.730 --> 00:38:31.610
yes, this can touch memory
that it shouldn't touch,

00:38:31.610 --> 00:38:35.940
or it can acquire the lock
and never release the lock.

00:38:35.940 --> 00:38:40.982
And so you see exactly what the
pattern is and how to fix that.

00:38:40.982 --> 00:38:42.440
Now, all this
proving technology is

00:38:42.440 --> 00:38:44.819
having a wonderful
effect on innovation

00:38:44.819 --> 00:38:45.860
in programming languages.

00:38:45.860 --> 00:38:47.510
Because we want
to take everything

00:38:47.510 --> 00:38:49.370
that the programmer
knows about the data

00:38:49.370 --> 00:38:51.770
types and the
constraints and express

00:38:51.770 --> 00:38:54.710
those in as high a level,
as strong in fashion as we

00:38:54.710 --> 00:38:56.660
can through contracts.

00:38:56.660 --> 00:39:00.020
And this idea of expressing
the contracts very easily,

00:39:00.020 --> 00:39:03.640
having languages that are very
explicit about those things--

00:39:03.640 --> 00:39:06.300
that takes all the theory
of language innovation

00:39:06.300 --> 00:39:08.990
and brings it into the
mainstream and says,

00:39:08.990 --> 00:39:13.070
we really need
those capabilities.

00:39:13.070 --> 00:39:17.810
Keeping systems up to date,
being able to look at a system

00:39:17.810 --> 00:39:20.120
and say, is its behavior normal?

00:39:20.120 --> 00:39:23.630
We need this both at the
single computer level

00:39:23.630 --> 00:39:24.820
and looking at the network.

00:39:24.820 --> 00:39:26.390
Is there a type
of traffic that's

00:39:26.390 --> 00:39:29.900
exploded in terms of
usage, at a time where

00:39:29.900 --> 00:39:33.590
the overall traffic is starting
to be too heavy for the network

00:39:33.590 --> 00:39:34.530
to do it?

00:39:34.530 --> 00:39:37.430
There should be automatic tools
out there that are doing that.

00:39:37.430 --> 00:39:40.220
And in fact, machine
learning techniques

00:39:40.220 --> 00:39:42.710
that build the model
of typical behavior.

00:39:42.710 --> 00:39:45.380
And then see these
things that are unusual

00:39:45.380 --> 00:39:47.990
will be used at every
level of the system--

00:39:47.990 --> 00:39:51.230
at the memory management
level, at the API level,

00:39:51.230 --> 00:39:55.820
at the network sending level,
and the wide network monitoring

00:39:55.820 --> 00:39:56.520
level.

00:39:56.520 --> 00:39:58.550
And we call that
behavior blocking.

00:39:58.550 --> 00:40:01.250
And that'll be a
critical component

00:40:01.250 --> 00:40:05.040
in solving those
security issues.

00:40:05.040 --> 00:40:06.860
Another set of
areas that I think

00:40:06.860 --> 00:40:09.500
are making wonderful
progress are

00:40:09.500 --> 00:40:13.730
getting a more natural interface
between users and the computer.

00:40:13.730 --> 00:40:15.920
Victor Zue here has been
a big advocate of that,

00:40:15.920 --> 00:40:17.420
building some
wonderful systems that

00:40:17.420 --> 00:40:20.690
take speech all the way
up into particular domains

00:40:20.690 --> 00:40:22.790
and let people
interact with those

00:40:22.790 --> 00:40:27.590
and making it so that it's
not a huge technical exercise

00:40:27.590 --> 00:40:29.690
to build one of those systems.

00:40:29.690 --> 00:40:32.390
That you just have a general
runtime for that, I think,

00:40:32.390 --> 00:40:36.000
is something that will be
solved in the years ahead.

00:40:36.000 --> 00:40:39.110
The prowess in speech
recognition is very good.

00:40:39.110 --> 00:40:42.650
If you take a isolated,
simplistic case

00:40:42.650 --> 00:40:46.190
where there's no
context and no noise

00:40:46.190 --> 00:40:48.260
and a perfect microphone--

00:40:48.260 --> 00:40:50.720
three great simplifications--
the difference

00:40:50.720 --> 00:40:54.410
between a human and a
computer is not very drastic.

00:40:54.410 --> 00:40:56.360
It's as we relax
those constraints

00:40:56.360 --> 00:40:58.580
and bring in context--

00:40:58.580 --> 00:41:00.920
crummy microphones and noise--

00:41:00.920 --> 00:41:05.960
that then the divergence between
the computer and the human

00:41:05.960 --> 00:41:07.810
is quite substantial.

00:41:07.810 --> 00:41:09.560
And of course, human
users of these things

00:41:09.560 --> 00:41:10.970
are very demanding.

00:41:10.970 --> 00:41:14.660
Because speech doesn't
operate at a conscious level,

00:41:14.660 --> 00:41:17.856
as it makes mistakes, you just
get irritated and talk louder.

00:41:17.856 --> 00:41:19.230
And of course,
it's been trained.

00:41:19.230 --> 00:41:21.200
It's learned you're
speaking in normal tones.

00:41:21.200 --> 00:41:23.030
So it just gets worse and worse.

00:41:23.030 --> 00:41:26.870
And so it just generates while
you're yelling at the system.

00:41:26.870 --> 00:41:29.750
Now, ink is not quite the same.

00:41:29.750 --> 00:41:31.970
It's a little easier
because you process ink

00:41:31.970 --> 00:41:33.560
at a conscious level.

00:41:33.560 --> 00:41:36.230
And so although it's irritating
when it doesn't work,

00:41:36.230 --> 00:41:39.500
you can look and say, well, how
could I have recognized that?

00:41:39.500 --> 00:41:43.610
Is that E looped so closely
that it looks like a C?

00:41:43.610 --> 00:41:47.390
And in fact, as we monitor
people using our handwriting

00:41:47.390 --> 00:41:50.900
system, the plasticity is
partly in our subsystem.

00:41:50.900 --> 00:41:53.870
But a lot of it's in
the user, that the user

00:41:53.870 --> 00:41:56.780
consciously or
subconsciously is actually

00:41:56.780 --> 00:42:01.280
writing more explicitly
the features that have

00:42:01.280 --> 00:42:03.240
caused the problem in the past.

00:42:03.240 --> 00:42:04.590
So that's one where--

00:42:04.590 --> 00:42:07.500
that's partly why ink is
coming into the mainstream,

00:42:07.500 --> 00:42:11.640
say, a few years in terms of
general input, the equivalent

00:42:11.640 --> 00:42:13.720
of dictation a
little bit faster.

00:42:13.720 --> 00:42:16.830
The place we're seeing our
speech work really catch on

00:42:16.830 --> 00:42:19.380
is a combination of people
where the keyboard is

00:42:19.380 --> 00:42:21.810
unattractive for
them for any reason,

00:42:21.810 --> 00:42:26.070
including repetitive stress
injury or people in China,

00:42:26.070 --> 00:42:29.190
Japan, or Korea, where the
keyboard is relatively less

00:42:29.190 --> 00:42:30.930
effective as an input technique.

00:42:30.930 --> 00:42:35.490
We can already beat the
fastest typist of Chinese

00:42:35.490 --> 00:42:38.560
with a Chinese speech
recognition system.

00:42:38.560 --> 00:42:42.150
And so now, that's a
milestone along the way

00:42:42.150 --> 00:42:45.190
that is pretty exciting.

00:42:45.190 --> 00:42:47.590
General artificial
intelligence-- this

00:42:47.590 --> 00:42:48.714
is the holy grail.

00:42:48.714 --> 00:42:50.380
And when I was talking
to faculty today,

00:42:50.380 --> 00:42:52.420
I was impressed
that MIT has kept

00:42:52.420 --> 00:42:56.710
its commitment to this area
and throughout all the years.

00:42:56.710 --> 00:43:00.520
And it is one where
some very interesting

00:43:00.520 --> 00:43:02.620
approaches--
statistical approaches,

00:43:02.620 --> 00:43:04.480
Bayesian approaches
are now starting

00:43:04.480 --> 00:43:07.690
to be used in
different fashions.

00:43:07.690 --> 00:43:10.690
The actual product on the
market that apparently

00:43:10.690 --> 00:43:13.510
is a spin-off related
to a professor--

00:43:13.510 --> 00:43:17.260
here's the thing that goes
around and vacuums the rug.

00:43:17.260 --> 00:43:20.830
And that's pretty low
level, better than nothing.

00:43:20.830 --> 00:43:25.900
But we want to move up in
terms of the things that go on.

00:43:25.900 --> 00:43:30.160
For gaming, one place we're
using our machine learning

00:43:30.160 --> 00:43:33.130
technology a lot is
we, on Xbox Live,

00:43:33.130 --> 00:43:36.760
can watch player behavior
and different strategies.

00:43:36.760 --> 00:43:38.245
And the machine can learn.

00:43:38.245 --> 00:43:40.810
And so if you want
to pick an opponent--

00:43:40.810 --> 00:43:43.150
typically if you play the
computer historically,

00:43:43.150 --> 00:43:47.110
it's a road algorithm that
isn't that much fun to play,

00:43:47.110 --> 00:43:52.870
because eventually you see
that as being very predictable.

00:43:52.870 --> 00:43:55.060
We can take all the
play styles that we're

00:43:55.060 --> 00:43:58.510
seeing across this
network and create

00:43:58.510 --> 00:44:02.320
any sort of level of difficulty
or different fashion of play

00:44:02.320 --> 00:44:06.460
and make it as interesting
and as varied as playing

00:44:06.460 --> 00:44:10.000
with human opponents,
including letting you win

00:44:10.000 --> 00:44:14.200
every once in a while,
which on video games for me,

00:44:14.200 --> 00:44:15.730
that is pretty tough.

00:44:15.730 --> 00:44:16.880
You pick these things up.

00:44:16.880 --> 00:44:18.500
And they are geared to--

00:44:18.500 --> 00:44:23.750
well, to you and not to people
who haven't used them nearly

00:44:23.750 --> 00:44:24.250
as much.

00:44:24.250 --> 00:44:26.112
And so we'll make
these things appeal.

00:44:26.112 --> 00:44:27.820
And even if you start
to beat the system,

00:44:27.820 --> 00:44:31.060
boy, we'll crank
it up to a level

00:44:31.060 --> 00:44:33.470
that will keep it challenging.

00:44:33.470 --> 00:44:36.220
So AI is going to be applied
in a lot of different ways--

00:44:36.220 --> 00:44:39.560
modeling things in
the other sciences

00:44:39.560 --> 00:44:42.460
happening with dynamic
behavior and systems--

00:44:42.460 --> 00:44:44.240
very, very important.

00:44:44.240 --> 00:44:47.720
All the natural interface
techniques-- vision, speech--

00:44:47.720 --> 00:44:52.450
we'll come to take those for
granted in a very strong way.

00:44:52.450 --> 00:44:54.250
Now, the boundary
between computer science

00:44:54.250 --> 00:44:56.260
and the other
sciences historically

00:44:56.260 --> 00:44:57.500
was a fairly hard boundary.

00:44:57.500 --> 00:45:00.760
And that is breaking down.

00:45:00.760 --> 00:45:02.740
One great example of
that is the research.

00:45:02.740 --> 00:45:05.500
We have Jim Gray who
looked at astronomy

00:45:05.500 --> 00:45:07.960
and said, boy, there's
a lot of data there.

00:45:07.960 --> 00:45:11.586
And a lot of the advances
come in proposing something

00:45:11.586 --> 00:45:13.210
that you can either
validate by looking

00:45:13.210 --> 00:45:15.640
at that data or invalidate.

00:45:15.640 --> 00:45:18.610
And so we really need to get
all these databases connected

00:45:18.610 --> 00:45:19.540
together.

00:45:19.540 --> 00:45:23.800
And the semantics and
our very high level--

00:45:23.800 --> 00:45:25.600
it's, again, not just
a relational problem.

00:45:25.600 --> 00:45:28.600
But collaborating with
a lot of astronomers

00:45:28.600 --> 00:45:31.660
who know the domain, he's been
hooking up those databases

00:45:31.660 --> 00:45:36.750
and now navigating through this
logically connected database--

00:45:36.750 --> 00:45:39.280
is a very important
tool in astronomy.

00:45:39.280 --> 00:45:43.600
And many of the sciences are
going to where those rich data

00:45:43.600 --> 00:45:47.020
collections are necessary
for everyone to have access

00:45:47.020 --> 00:45:48.730
to in a high-level way.

00:45:48.730 --> 00:45:51.730
Biology, of course, is perhaps
one of the most challenging,

00:45:51.730 --> 00:45:54.230
because of the breadth and
the differences in the data.

00:45:54.230 --> 00:45:56.700
But even there, this
is starting to happen.

00:45:56.700 --> 00:45:58.714
And I had a great
discussion with some

00:45:58.714 --> 00:46:00.880
of the faculty who are
pushing off in that direction

00:46:00.880 --> 00:46:04.420
and really seen that the
boundary of computer science

00:46:04.420 --> 00:46:08.230
and biology are very
much coming together.

00:46:08.230 --> 00:46:11.110
And we need people who
understand both of those things

00:46:11.110 --> 00:46:15.160
to make advances in
solving diseases.

00:46:15.160 --> 00:46:17.860
And I'm very optimistic
about how fast

00:46:17.860 --> 00:46:19.360
that will move forward.

00:46:19.360 --> 00:46:21.250
Now, this tool that we've got--

00:46:21.250 --> 00:46:24.050
the PC connected to
the internet and all

00:46:24.050 --> 00:46:28.870
this great empowerment-- it's
such an important thing that we

00:46:28.870 --> 00:46:31.900
do have to worry that there
are people who are benefiting

00:46:31.900 --> 00:46:33.590
from this and people are not.

00:46:33.590 --> 00:46:36.520
And people talk about that
as the digital divide.

00:46:36.520 --> 00:46:39.310
It's something that I think
people in computer science

00:46:39.310 --> 00:46:42.730
should care a lot about
and in various ways

00:46:42.730 --> 00:46:47.020
contribute to trying to
minimize that difference.

00:46:47.020 --> 00:46:49.690
One of the projects
that Microsoft

00:46:49.690 --> 00:46:52.120
got involved with together
with my foundation

00:46:52.120 --> 00:46:54.670
was saying, well,
what about libraries?

00:46:54.670 --> 00:46:57.310
Would it be appropriate
to have computers there?

00:46:57.310 --> 00:46:59.350
And we were a little
worried about this,

00:46:59.350 --> 00:47:02.200
because would the
librarian like it?

00:47:02.200 --> 00:47:04.360
Would it be at the
expense of the books?

00:47:04.360 --> 00:47:08.970
Would kids come in and
just hack up the machines?

00:47:08.970 --> 00:47:12.370
Would they be doing
enrichening things

00:47:12.370 --> 00:47:14.230
as they were using
those computers?

00:47:14.230 --> 00:47:15.070
It was unclear.

00:47:15.070 --> 00:47:16.960
But six years ago,
we kicked it off.

00:47:16.960 --> 00:47:18.670
We did pilot projects.

00:47:18.670 --> 00:47:24.270
And over six years in all 50
states in 18,000 libraries

00:47:24.270 --> 00:47:25.690
in a rural--

00:47:25.690 --> 00:47:29.670
everywhere in the country, we
put in over 50,000 computers.

00:47:29.670 --> 00:47:32.770
And the response of
librarians was just phenomenal

00:47:32.770 --> 00:47:35.980
of wanting to be trained,
wanting to reinforce

00:47:35.980 --> 00:47:38.230
the role of that library.

00:47:38.230 --> 00:47:41.500
And traffic to the library
increased, not just to come

00:47:41.500 --> 00:47:44.800
use the computer, but
also the number of books

00:47:44.800 --> 00:47:46.030
that were being lent out.

00:47:46.030 --> 00:47:48.070
And we were able to
monitor and support

00:47:48.070 --> 00:47:51.670
all these things in a
very efficient way that

00:47:51.670 --> 00:47:53.161
made it work very well.

00:47:53.161 --> 00:47:54.910
Throughout the project,
we learned things.

00:47:54.910 --> 00:47:56.659
We came up with a
version of the software.

00:47:56.659 --> 00:48:00.100
You can just hit a button to
switch from Spanish to English

00:48:00.100 --> 00:48:01.720
for a lot of these libraries.

00:48:01.720 --> 00:48:05.750
We had a button you can just
hit to switch to big print.

00:48:05.750 --> 00:48:08.080
So if you don't like
reading the fonts

00:48:08.080 --> 00:48:09.430
that we typically use-- boom.

00:48:09.430 --> 00:48:12.670
All of a sudden,
it's a lot better.

00:48:12.670 --> 00:48:14.530
We came up with
things to help people

00:48:14.530 --> 00:48:15.760
with the common scenarios.

00:48:15.760 --> 00:48:19.360
And so it's great to see if
you give people those tools,

00:48:19.360 --> 00:48:20.970
they'll use them.

00:48:20.970 --> 00:48:23.770
And it really makes a
big difference for them.

00:48:23.770 --> 00:48:25.720
Getting these tools
out to schools,

00:48:25.720 --> 00:48:28.120
getting them out to all
different countries-- a lot

00:48:28.120 --> 00:48:33.147
of challenges remain there
that need to be addressed.

00:48:33.147 --> 00:48:34.480
We think about computer science.

00:48:34.480 --> 00:48:38.069
One thing computer science
has done through the internet,

00:48:38.069 --> 00:48:40.360
through software-- it's made
the world a smaller place.

00:48:40.360 --> 00:48:43.120
In fact, people
now worry that this

00:48:43.120 --> 00:48:45.880
is going to create a new
level of global competition.

00:48:45.880 --> 00:48:47.710
And the answer is it is.

00:48:47.710 --> 00:48:52.210
People's opportunity to have
great jobs in the future

00:48:52.210 --> 00:48:56.020
will be far more determined
by their level of education

00:48:56.020 --> 00:48:58.600
than by what country
they happen to be in.

00:48:58.600 --> 00:49:03.730
Historically, your education
level didn't matter that much.

00:49:03.730 --> 00:49:06.230
If you were in a rich country,
you made a lot of money.

00:49:06.230 --> 00:49:07.250
And if you were
in a poor country,

00:49:07.250 --> 00:49:08.410
you made very little money.

00:49:08.410 --> 00:49:11.350
Now, the opportunity
for educated people

00:49:11.350 --> 00:49:15.010
worldwide to help out, to
contribute to products--

00:49:15.010 --> 00:49:18.190
not just software products,
but anything you can imagine--

00:49:18.190 --> 00:49:21.340
architecture, law,
answering the phone--

00:49:21.340 --> 00:49:24.790
it will be done where
people have those skills.

00:49:24.790 --> 00:49:28.370
And as people look at that, they
go, wow, what does that mean?

00:49:28.370 --> 00:49:31.330
Well, it means the US has
to keep its edge in terms

00:49:31.330 --> 00:49:33.001
of doing the best work.

00:49:33.001 --> 00:49:34.000
And that means research.

00:49:34.000 --> 00:49:35.335
It means intellectual property.

00:49:35.335 --> 00:49:39.940
It means improving the
education system, rededication.

00:49:39.940 --> 00:49:42.160
It's very similar to what
happened in the 1980s

00:49:42.160 --> 00:49:45.370
when there was a lot
of angst about Japan.

00:49:45.370 --> 00:49:47.410
Japan at the time
appeared to have

00:49:47.410 --> 00:49:50.170
a model where they would just
pick an industry-- the car

00:49:50.170 --> 00:49:52.720
industry, the computer industry,
the consumer electronics

00:49:52.720 --> 00:49:53.410
industry.

00:49:53.410 --> 00:49:56.560
And boom, they
would do it better.

00:49:56.560 --> 00:49:58.330
And the great
thing that happened

00:49:58.330 --> 00:50:01.150
in the '80s was there was a lot
of humility, a lot of thinking,

00:50:01.150 --> 00:50:05.380
well, do we just match what
they do exactly that way?

00:50:05.380 --> 00:50:08.560
Or do we just push
forward on our strengths,

00:50:08.560 --> 00:50:10.480
our approach to things?

00:50:10.480 --> 00:50:15.400
During the '80s, they
did this AI project.

00:50:15.400 --> 00:50:18.020
And it really-- because
of the way it was done,

00:50:18.020 --> 00:50:20.342
it wasn't done in the
diverse academic approach

00:50:20.342 --> 00:50:21.050
that we use here.

00:50:21.050 --> 00:50:24.070
It really ended up
not generating much.

00:50:24.070 --> 00:50:25.480
So we dedicate ourselves.

00:50:25.480 --> 00:50:28.150
And it's actually the work
done during that period that

00:50:28.150 --> 00:50:31.290
led to that
productivity increase

00:50:31.290 --> 00:50:34.240
that benefited all
countries, but the US

00:50:34.240 --> 00:50:36.240
in particular during the 1990s.

00:50:36.240 --> 00:50:38.860
And I see that same
thing repeating itself

00:50:38.860 --> 00:50:43.150
as we question our
unique role and reinforce

00:50:43.150 --> 00:50:45.560
what needs to be done.

00:50:45.560 --> 00:50:49.700
One challenge that we have
in the field-- in all science

00:50:49.700 --> 00:50:52.550
fields, but particularly
in computer science

00:50:52.550 --> 00:50:54.440
is the issue of diversity.

00:50:54.440 --> 00:50:58.510
To do the best work, we want
to draw on everybody's talent

00:50:58.510 --> 00:51:01.940
and give everybody
a deep involvement.

00:51:01.940 --> 00:51:05.180
The variety of jobs, the
need for great people

00:51:05.180 --> 00:51:07.160
is pretty phenomenal.

00:51:07.160 --> 00:51:09.860
And the diversity numbers
in some professions

00:51:09.860 --> 00:51:14.600
like law and medicine have
been going pretty strongly

00:51:14.600 --> 00:51:16.410
in the favorable direction.

00:51:16.410 --> 00:51:20.210
One thing I've personally gotten
involved with to try and help

00:51:20.210 --> 00:51:23.030
push this forward is a
scholarship program that's

00:51:23.030 --> 00:51:26.000
called the Gates
Millennium Scholarship.

00:51:26.000 --> 00:51:29.570
And here at MIT-- out of
actually a thousand people

00:51:29.570 --> 00:51:32.610
who get those scholarships,
60 people are here at MIT.

00:51:32.610 --> 00:51:38.000
So it's a great thing and
a real endorsement of MIT,

00:51:38.000 --> 00:51:41.150
that there are more Gates
Scholars at this school

00:51:41.150 --> 00:51:43.840
than at any other school.

00:51:43.840 --> 00:51:45.980
Now, these science
problems are tough,

00:51:45.980 --> 00:51:47.480
but they're fun to work on.

00:51:47.480 --> 00:51:50.080
The jobs that are
involved with them are--

00:51:50.080 --> 00:51:51.300
you can have an impact.

00:51:51.300 --> 00:51:53.010
You can work with other people.

00:51:53.010 --> 00:51:56.820
It's not just somebody
isolated, off coding all night,

00:51:56.820 --> 00:51:59.040
although if you want to
do that, that's fine.

00:51:59.040 --> 00:52:01.850
We still have lots of
jobs that are like that.

00:52:01.850 --> 00:52:06.890
And so the sense of
reward, being involved

00:52:06.890 --> 00:52:09.350
in changing business,
changing entertainment,

00:52:09.350 --> 00:52:14.220
changing education, giving tools
to those new science sciences,

00:52:14.220 --> 00:52:16.260
including to help the disease--

00:52:16.260 --> 00:52:18.630
I think that's a
phenomenal opportunity.

00:52:18.630 --> 00:52:22.280
And so that's why I'm more
excited about computer science

00:52:22.280 --> 00:52:23.180
than ever.

00:52:23.180 --> 00:52:26.390
And I'm very excited
to see what some of you

00:52:26.390 --> 00:52:29.900
here can do, taking
that to the next level.

00:52:29.900 --> 00:52:30.750
Thank you.

00:52:30.750 --> 00:52:32.220
[APPLAUSE]

00:52:49.010 --> 00:52:50.930
MODERATOR: Thank you very much--

00:52:50.930 --> 00:52:53.426
interesting and fun.

00:52:53.426 --> 00:52:55.550
Now, maybe we come to
something-- will also be fun.

00:52:55.550 --> 00:52:58.192
You'll all get a chance
to ask him questions.

00:52:58.192 --> 00:53:01.108
I'd like to get as many
questions in as possible.

00:53:01.108 --> 00:53:03.052
There are microphones
in each aisle.

00:53:03.052 --> 00:53:05.305
So if you want to ask a
question, go to a microphone.

00:53:05.305 --> 00:53:06.700
And two up, please.

00:53:06.700 --> 00:53:09.220
And do me a favor.

00:53:09.220 --> 00:53:11.830
Have your question end
with a question mark.

00:53:11.830 --> 00:53:13.870
So it should actually
be a question

00:53:13.870 --> 00:53:16.807
and try and keep it
as short as you can.

00:53:16.807 --> 00:53:18.640
And then when you're
done, go take your seat

00:53:18.640 --> 00:53:21.250
and give Bill a
chance to answer it.

00:53:21.250 --> 00:53:22.866
You're up.

00:53:22.866 --> 00:53:24.490
AUDIENCE: Could you
explain to me .NET?

00:53:24.490 --> 00:53:26.890
I've seen a lot of
things about .NET--

00:53:26.890 --> 00:53:28.630
Never really understood
what it does,

00:53:28.630 --> 00:53:31.935
what it actually accomplishes.

00:53:31.935 --> 00:53:35.980
GATES: Well, there's a
revolution in how information

00:53:35.980 --> 00:53:38.050
is represented around XML.

00:53:38.050 --> 00:53:41.050
And that's something that
we and a few small companies

00:53:41.050 --> 00:53:43.810
got behind, now seven
or eight years ago.

00:53:43.810 --> 00:53:47.530
And in the year 2000, we said we
were really betting our company

00:53:47.530 --> 00:53:50.350
on taking those
XML representations

00:53:50.350 --> 00:53:53.020
and having some protocols
around them that

00:53:53.020 --> 00:53:56.080
are now called the web
services protocols, making

00:53:56.080 --> 00:53:57.010
those standards.

00:53:57.010 --> 00:53:59.620
Standards that would
exist on any operating

00:53:59.620 --> 00:54:02.080
system, any language
and letting those

00:54:02.080 --> 00:54:05.230
be the basis for
deep interoperability

00:54:05.230 --> 00:54:09.970
for applications like
e-commerce that I talked about.

00:54:09.970 --> 00:54:15.040
And so .NET is us embracing
XML, embracing web services,

00:54:15.040 --> 00:54:17.280
and building that into
the Windows platform.

00:54:17.280 --> 00:54:21.460
And it means building web
services and XML into Excel,

00:54:21.460 --> 00:54:24.140
building it into the
SQL Server database,

00:54:24.140 --> 00:54:26.560
having Windows use that to
be the way that you set it

00:54:26.560 --> 00:54:29.180
up and manage it,
so that management

00:54:29.180 --> 00:54:31.870
isn't a set of
protocols, or approaches,

00:54:31.870 --> 00:54:34.870
or APIs that are separate
or off to the side.

00:54:34.870 --> 00:54:38.740
So bringing .NET to all of our
products and connecting it up

00:54:38.740 --> 00:54:43.150
this way is something that's
a very broad project that

00:54:43.150 --> 00:54:47.350
we're about 60% of
the way done with.

00:54:47.350 --> 00:54:50.186
And when we made that bet, it
looked like a very risky bet.

00:54:50.186 --> 00:54:52.810
But I have to say at this point
with the cooperation on the web

00:54:52.810 --> 00:54:56.910
services standards, the
excitement around XML--

00:54:56.910 --> 00:55:00.205
it's not a bet that's
risky at all at this point.

00:55:00.205 --> 00:55:01.257
AUDIENCE: Thanks.

00:55:01.257 --> 00:55:02.090
MODERATOR: Thank you

00:55:02.090 --> 00:55:03.860
AUDIENCE: Thank you for coming.

00:55:03.860 --> 00:55:07.220
If you were 19 years old again
and let's say this time around,

00:55:07.220 --> 00:55:11.630
today you're at MIT, and
Microsoft was already founded.

00:55:11.630 --> 00:55:13.820
Would you continue
your education

00:55:13.820 --> 00:55:16.610
and/or create a new company?

00:55:16.610 --> 00:55:19.340
And what type of
company and why?

00:55:19.340 --> 00:55:22.325
GATES: Well, I loved
going to school.

00:55:26.770 --> 00:55:29.420
MODERATOR: He didn't go to MIT.

00:55:29.420 --> 00:55:31.080
GATES: But it was very similar.

00:55:31.080 --> 00:55:34.940
There were very smart people
around to talk to every day.

00:55:34.940 --> 00:55:38.630
They fed you every day.

00:55:38.630 --> 00:55:41.704
You didn't have
to go to classes.

00:55:41.704 --> 00:55:43.120
If you worked a
little bit, they'd

00:55:43.120 --> 00:55:44.880
give you this positive
reinforcement,

00:55:44.880 --> 00:55:48.620
these grades every once in
a while to encourage you.

00:55:48.620 --> 00:55:52.090
And so what had
happened was my friend,

00:55:52.090 --> 00:55:54.910
Paul Allen, who saw
this microprocessor

00:55:54.910 --> 00:55:59.260
and really egged me on,
had come out to Boston

00:55:59.260 --> 00:56:01.330
and taken a job out
here at Honeywell

00:56:01.330 --> 00:56:04.402
to say to me every day, it's
time to start our company,

00:56:04.402 --> 00:56:05.610
it's time to start a company.

00:56:05.610 --> 00:56:09.220
Well, he didn't
succeed until he had

00:56:09.220 --> 00:56:12.790
that cover of Popular
Electronics and said,

00:56:12.790 --> 00:56:15.370
it's going to start without us.

00:56:15.370 --> 00:56:16.990
Let's be the first.

00:56:16.990 --> 00:56:19.850
And so then officially,
I didn't leave school.

00:56:19.850 --> 00:56:21.702
I went on leave.

00:56:21.702 --> 00:56:22.660
And I'm still on leave.

00:56:22.660 --> 00:56:23.995
[LAUGHTER]

00:56:25.330 --> 00:56:28.270
In fact, when Harvard has my
name in their publications,

00:56:28.270 --> 00:56:32.930
they always put '77 after
it as though I graduated,

00:56:32.930 --> 00:56:34.630
which of course, I didn't.

00:56:34.630 --> 00:56:37.810
And so I'd say that if
I was a student today,

00:56:37.810 --> 00:56:43.000
I'd certainly be looking
for some paradigm shift that

00:56:43.000 --> 00:56:45.550
could make a dramatic change.

00:56:45.550 --> 00:56:50.020
I don't know if I'd work in
AI, computational biology.

00:56:50.020 --> 00:56:51.940
Those are two that
I really love.

00:56:51.940 --> 00:56:53.970
And I'm glad to hear
those are-- there's

00:56:53.970 --> 00:56:58.600
a strong increase in emphasis
in those things here at MIT.

00:56:58.600 --> 00:57:02.500
But I'd say that anyone who has
in mind a deep paradigm shift

00:57:02.500 --> 00:57:05.590
for an industry, go ahead.

00:57:05.590 --> 00:57:06.760
Take the risk.

00:57:06.760 --> 00:57:07.960
Go away for a couple years.

00:57:07.960 --> 00:57:10.540
And MIT will probably
let you come back

00:57:10.540 --> 00:57:15.790
if it wasn't the paradigm
shifting thing that you'd

00:57:15.790 --> 00:57:16.965
hoped it would be.

00:57:16.965 --> 00:57:17.815
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:57:17.815 --> 00:57:19.090
MODERATOR: Thank you.

00:57:19.090 --> 00:57:21.340
AUDIENCE: Many people
consider you an inspiration.

00:57:21.340 --> 00:57:23.841
Who is your personal hero?

00:57:23.841 --> 00:57:25.272
[LAUGHTER]

00:57:26.230 --> 00:57:28.030
GATES: I have many heroes--

00:57:28.030 --> 00:57:34.040
people that I've been
lucky enough to work with,

00:57:34.040 --> 00:57:37.610
from my parents to a lot
of people at Microsoft.

00:57:37.610 --> 00:57:42.170
One that I'd really
highlight is Warren Buffett.

00:57:42.170 --> 00:57:45.320
His way of looking
at the world, looking

00:57:45.320 --> 00:57:49.280
at how business should be done,
showing that you can have fun

00:57:49.280 --> 00:57:53.510
while doing a very
important, tough job.

00:57:53.510 --> 00:57:55.910
He's had an incredible
influence on me.

00:57:55.910 --> 00:57:57.950
And it's part of the
reason I say, hey,

00:57:57.950 --> 00:58:00.050
I have the best job in
the world, because I can--

00:58:00.050 --> 00:58:02.230
I have had a chance to
meet somebody like Warren

00:58:02.230 --> 00:58:05.970
and get his advice
and his humor,

00:58:05.970 --> 00:58:10.220
which is really, really unique.

00:58:10.220 --> 00:58:11.300
MODERATOR: Please.

00:58:11.300 --> 00:58:13.490
AUDIENCE: Hi, how do
you feel about software

00:58:13.490 --> 00:58:17.870
patents and their role in
either promoting or stifling

00:58:17.870 --> 00:58:18.460
innovation?

00:58:18.460 --> 00:58:21.230
And are you concerned
about them long term?

00:58:21.230 --> 00:58:24.530
I know that Microsoft got sued
a little while ago for that.

00:58:24.530 --> 00:58:25.030
[INAUDIBLE]

00:58:25.030 --> 00:58:25.370
MODERATOR: Really?

00:58:25.370 --> 00:58:26.330
GATES: Oh, we got sued.

00:58:29.370 --> 00:58:32.120
It's interesting because
we're on really both sides

00:58:32.120 --> 00:58:33.920
of this patent thing.

00:58:33.920 --> 00:58:37.190
We, because of our
success, are a target

00:58:37.190 --> 00:58:42.120
for patents, some of them not
as good as they should be.

00:58:42.120 --> 00:58:46.020
And we're also a company that
files lots and lots of patents.

00:58:46.020 --> 00:58:49.400
We file more software patents
than any other company.

00:58:49.400 --> 00:58:51.440
The patent system
is not perfect.

00:58:51.440 --> 00:58:56.390
And I think any freshman could
look at the patent system

00:58:56.390 --> 00:58:59.540
and suggest improvements
to the patent system.

00:58:59.540 --> 00:59:04.230
But it's a system that
has worked amazingly well,

00:59:04.230 --> 00:59:06.380
despite its mistakes
and its problems.

00:59:06.380 --> 00:59:08.300
Over several hundred
years, starting

00:59:08.300 --> 00:59:13.430
with Benjamin Franklin, the kind
of incentives for invention,

00:59:13.430 --> 00:59:15.680
the creation of the
biotechnology field--

00:59:15.680 --> 00:59:16.820
is that a good thing?

00:59:16.820 --> 00:59:19.020
The pharmaceutical industry--
is that a good thing?

00:59:19.020 --> 00:59:22.640
These are industries that the
US leads in and have created

00:59:22.640 --> 00:59:27.020
immense improvement in human
welfare and all based on just

00:59:27.020 --> 00:59:30.710
taking the patent system
as it was and using it,

00:59:30.710 --> 00:59:33.200
so that as they would
do innovative work,

00:59:33.200 --> 00:59:36.140
there would be some
incentive there.

00:59:36.140 --> 00:59:41.030
And so I think we absolutely
can improve the patent system,

00:59:41.030 --> 00:59:43.230
but it's something
that is important.

00:59:43.230 --> 00:59:46.760
And particularly if we look at
global competition, the things

00:59:46.760 --> 00:59:51.560
that generate jobs here or help
the US be a strong leader--

00:59:51.560 --> 00:59:53.810
a lot of that is that if
we're super innovative,

00:59:53.810 --> 00:59:56.945
there's a reward
that goes with it.

00:59:56.945 --> 00:59:59.320
AUDIENCE: All right, thanks.

00:59:59.320 --> 01:00:01.450
AUDIENCE: So there's a
really interesting trend

01:00:01.450 --> 01:00:03.100
that I think you
started to touch on,

01:00:03.100 --> 01:00:04.870
but you didn't dive
very fully into,

01:00:04.870 --> 01:00:07.690
which is the commoditization
of software and software

01:00:07.690 --> 01:00:08.990
innovation.

01:00:08.990 --> 01:00:11.200
And what I was wondering
was, how do you

01:00:11.200 --> 01:00:14.590
see Microsoft kind of
fitting into a world where

01:00:14.590 --> 01:00:17.870
software is cheap and
produced at very low cost?

01:00:17.870 --> 01:00:21.010
And what do you have to say to
your current and prospective

01:00:21.010 --> 01:00:22.810
fleet of software engineers?

01:00:22.810 --> 01:00:23.956
GATES: Current?

01:00:23.956 --> 01:00:25.604
AUDIENCE: Current
and prospective fleet

01:00:25.604 --> 01:00:26.520
of software engineers.

01:00:26.520 --> 01:00:28.610
GATES: Oh, great.

01:00:28.610 --> 01:00:31.030
Well, yes.

01:00:31.030 --> 01:00:34.280
Software has this
interesting property

01:00:34.280 --> 01:00:37.500
that unfortunately
it never wears out.

01:00:37.500 --> 01:00:42.530
And so when we sell somebody
a copy of Windows and Office,

01:00:42.530 --> 01:00:45.620
they can just use it forever.

01:00:45.620 --> 01:00:47.400
And they don't owe us a dime.

01:00:47.400 --> 01:00:47.930
It's theirs.

01:00:47.930 --> 01:00:49.520
They get to use it.

01:00:49.520 --> 01:00:53.900
And so all we get paid ever--

01:00:53.900 --> 01:00:56.590
it's not like Coke where
they get thirsty again.

01:00:56.590 --> 01:00:59.480
That's a good business.

01:00:59.480 --> 01:01:02.980
But it's not as fun to be
in a business like that.

01:01:02.980 --> 01:01:05.690
All we get paid for
is our breakthroughs.

01:01:05.690 --> 01:01:08.210
We have to come up with a
version of Office that's

01:01:08.210 --> 01:01:10.940
worth licensing,
worth installing,

01:01:10.940 --> 01:01:15.660
worth learning, worth dealing
with some effect it might have,

01:01:15.660 --> 01:01:18.320
or it might change things.

01:01:18.320 --> 01:01:20.840
And that's our
economic proposition

01:01:20.840 --> 01:01:24.080
versus all the free
software that's out there,

01:01:24.080 --> 01:01:27.512
all the installed base of our
best work of the years past

01:01:27.512 --> 01:01:28.220
that's out there.

01:01:28.220 --> 01:01:31.110
And people don't have
to pay us a thing for.

01:01:31.110 --> 01:01:35.292
We have to innovate enough that
it's worthwhile to license.

01:01:35.292 --> 01:01:37.250
Now, there's another way
to look at it, though,

01:01:37.250 --> 01:01:41.390
which is if you take a knowledge
worker most anywhere, even

01:01:41.390 --> 01:01:47.450
in Bangalore and Hyderabad,
they're making $40,000 a year.

01:01:47.450 --> 01:01:48.890
They've got an office.

01:01:48.890 --> 01:01:50.260
They've got phone bills.

01:01:50.260 --> 01:01:52.610
They've even got paper clips.

01:01:52.610 --> 01:01:56.450
What we're saying to people
is, hey, pay us less than $100

01:01:56.450 --> 01:01:59.480
a year for the
right of that person

01:01:59.480 --> 01:02:04.590
to have the very best software
to communicate, create,

01:02:04.590 --> 01:02:06.370
collaborate,
organize their thing.

01:02:06.370 --> 01:02:08.320
So it's a percentage
of what it costs

01:02:08.320 --> 01:02:10.380
you to have that
information worker,

01:02:10.380 --> 01:02:13.580
to have the very up to date,
the very best software.

01:02:13.580 --> 01:02:19.290
Is it worth something less
than $100 a year to do that?

01:02:19.290 --> 01:02:23.550
And when we hire
people, we say, hey,

01:02:23.550 --> 01:02:26.060
if you don't think we can
do that, you shouldn't join,

01:02:26.060 --> 01:02:30.860
because that's the standard
we're held to every year.

01:02:30.860 --> 01:02:34.100
And so far, we've done okay.

01:02:34.100 --> 01:02:35.720
And certainly, the customers--

01:02:35.720 --> 01:02:38.011
when I mean the customers,
I've never been in a meeting

01:02:38.011 --> 01:02:39.710
where they say, you're done.

01:02:39.710 --> 01:02:41.540
You're done.

01:02:41.540 --> 01:02:44.120
They say, hey, your stuff isn't
easy enough to administer.

01:02:44.120 --> 01:02:45.350
It's not secure enough.

01:02:45.350 --> 01:02:47.030
It doesn't do this feature.

01:02:47.030 --> 01:02:49.940
It doesn't let us do this
customization that we need.

01:02:49.940 --> 01:02:53.240
So the need for better software
and the impact of better

01:02:53.240 --> 01:02:57.080
software is super, super high.

01:02:57.080 --> 01:02:59.840
And the fact that we've created
this high-volume, low-cost

01:02:59.840 --> 01:03:02.070
model works very well.

01:03:02.070 --> 01:03:04.437
The fact that once
we solve workflow,

01:03:04.437 --> 01:03:06.020
there's hundreds of
millions of people

01:03:06.020 --> 01:03:07.190
who will benefit from that.

01:03:07.190 --> 01:03:09.830
Once we get the spreadsheet
up to this modeling level,

01:03:09.830 --> 01:03:12.500
hundreds of millions of
people will benefit from that.

01:03:12.500 --> 01:03:17.593
It's a model that has worked
and that I believe in.

01:03:17.593 --> 01:03:19.777
AUDIENCE: Thanks.

01:03:19.777 --> 01:03:21.360
AUDIENCE: You gave
us a broad overview

01:03:21.360 --> 01:03:23.179
of many different
aspects of where you

01:03:23.179 --> 01:03:24.470
see things going in the future.

01:03:24.470 --> 01:03:26.136
I wanted to focus on
the digital divide.

01:03:28.800 --> 01:03:31.350
How did you-- there are a
lot of people with money

01:03:31.350 --> 01:03:33.380
that don't do nearly
as much as you do.

01:03:33.380 --> 01:03:36.360
I'm wondering why you found
that very important-- if there

01:03:36.360 --> 01:03:37.560
was a specific experience.

01:03:37.560 --> 01:03:40.759
And what's your concrete
vision for Microsoft

01:03:40.759 --> 01:03:42.300
and for your
foundation, for bridging

01:03:42.300 --> 01:03:44.640
that divide between the
US and other countries

01:03:44.640 --> 01:03:46.432
around the world--
developing countries?

01:03:46.432 --> 01:03:52.270
GATES: Well, Microsoft-- its
philanthropy focuses really

01:03:52.270 --> 01:03:53.660
in two areas.

01:03:53.660 --> 01:03:58.060
One is that whatever causes
our employees believe in,

01:03:58.060 --> 01:04:00.490
we match their donations.

01:04:00.490 --> 01:04:03.280
So if they want to
give to MIT, they

01:04:03.280 --> 01:04:06.880
want to give to
some local church.

01:04:06.880 --> 01:04:10.600
Whatever non-profit, we
match that, including they

01:04:10.600 --> 01:04:12.250
can take company
software and provide

01:04:12.250 --> 01:04:14.350
that at extremely low cost.

01:04:14.350 --> 01:04:15.460
The other thing is--

01:04:15.460 --> 01:04:17.397
the other focus
is digital divide.

01:04:17.397 --> 01:04:19.480
And this means going to
every country and thinking

01:04:19.480 --> 01:04:23.170
about, how can we get
computers to be available?

01:04:23.170 --> 01:04:26.440
It means the Boys &amp; Girls Club--
the thing we've done in the US.

01:04:26.440 --> 01:04:29.200
The library thing
we've done in the US.

01:04:29.200 --> 01:04:31.060
There's a group
called Empower that

01:04:31.060 --> 01:04:34.940
provides software to non-profits
and lets them do those things.

01:04:34.940 --> 01:04:38.500
So for Microsoft, it's
very much digital divide.

01:04:38.500 --> 01:04:41.380
My foundation is a
little bit different.

01:04:41.380 --> 01:04:43.060
And for Microsoft,
why do we do it?

01:04:43.060 --> 01:04:45.910
Well, our employees love it.

01:04:45.910 --> 01:04:48.040
It speaks to our
mission of empowerment,

01:04:48.040 --> 01:04:52.840
that it's not just empowerment
for people who are wealthy.

01:04:52.840 --> 01:04:54.790
It's empowerment for everyone.

01:04:54.790 --> 01:04:56.060
So it's a great morale thing.

01:04:56.060 --> 01:04:59.050
They get deeply
involved, and it builds

01:04:59.050 --> 01:05:02.030
relationships with
governments and other people

01:05:02.030 --> 01:05:03.900
we want to work with.

01:05:03.900 --> 01:05:07.500
For me personally, the
focus is a little broader.

01:05:07.500 --> 01:05:10.320
Because I didn't
want my philanthropy

01:05:10.320 --> 01:05:13.500
to be just related
to that one thing.

01:05:13.500 --> 01:05:18.090
And I thought I wouldn't do
philanthropy until I was old.

01:05:18.090 --> 01:05:21.210
I thought, okay, I'm going
to work and make money.

01:05:21.210 --> 01:05:25.435
And then I'll stop doing that,
and then I'll give money away.

01:05:25.435 --> 01:05:27.060
And I knew I was
going to give it away.

01:05:27.060 --> 01:05:29.760
Because I don't
believe that passing

01:05:29.760 --> 01:05:32.100
substantial sums of
money to your children

01:05:32.100 --> 01:05:34.600
is necessarily good for them.

01:05:34.600 --> 01:05:35.450
They may not agree.

01:05:38.539 --> 01:05:40.080
In fact, there's
one story about that

01:05:40.080 --> 01:05:45.050
where I was in bed this
weekend at 7:00 in the morning.

01:05:45.050 --> 01:05:49.080
And my daughter comes in and
wakes me up and says, dad, dad.

01:05:49.080 --> 01:05:50.130
I was using the computer.

01:05:50.130 --> 01:05:51.090
You got to come.

01:05:51.090 --> 01:05:52.798
I said, no, just keep
using the computer.

01:05:52.798 --> 01:05:54.350
It's okay.

01:05:54.350 --> 01:05:56.100
No, you got to come.

01:05:56.100 --> 01:05:58.716
I won some money.

01:05:58.716 --> 01:05:59.340
And so we went.

01:05:59.340 --> 01:06:01.879
And of course, it was
one of those contests

01:06:01.879 --> 01:06:03.420
that you really
hadn't won any money.

01:06:03.420 --> 01:06:05.671
They wanted her to
visit the website.

01:06:05.671 --> 01:06:08.170
And it made me think, geez, we
got to get rid of that stuff.

01:06:11.070 --> 01:06:13.650
So I had chosen that
my resources are

01:06:13.650 --> 01:06:17.420
going to go back to
society and in the best way

01:06:17.420 --> 01:06:18.420
that I can come up with.

01:06:18.420 --> 01:06:20.336
And that's turned out
to really be two things.

01:06:20.336 --> 01:06:22.989
One is world health.

01:06:22.989 --> 01:06:24.030
That's the biggest thing.

01:06:24.030 --> 01:06:26.190
That's in my view the
most urgent problem

01:06:26.190 --> 01:06:27.630
in the world at large--

01:06:27.630 --> 01:06:28.440
very unaddressed.

01:06:28.440 --> 01:06:31.830
I could speak for
days about that.

01:06:31.830 --> 01:06:33.930
And then second is
the biggest problem

01:06:33.930 --> 01:06:35.940
in this country,
which is education.

01:06:35.940 --> 01:06:38.007
And that's where the
library program--

01:06:38.007 --> 01:06:39.840
and the Gates Millennium
Scholarship Program

01:06:39.840 --> 01:06:41.160
comes out of that.

01:06:41.160 --> 01:06:44.310
And because of the urgency
of those two problems,

01:06:44.310 --> 01:06:47.740
I've ended up doing something
I didn't expect to do,

01:06:47.740 --> 01:06:50.670
which is I have some days
where part of the day

01:06:50.670 --> 01:06:51.931
I'm trying to make money.

01:06:51.931 --> 01:06:53.805
And then part of the
day, I'm giving it away.

01:06:53.805 --> 01:06:57.900
And so far it hasn't made me
schizophrenic to try and bridge

01:06:57.900 --> 01:07:00.778
those two [INAUDIBLE].

01:07:00.778 --> 01:07:01.470
MODERATOR: Yeah?

01:07:01.470 --> 01:07:03.470
AUDIENCE: I had a question
relating to security.

01:07:03.470 --> 01:07:04.928
I guess it's a
great responsibility

01:07:04.928 --> 01:07:07.550
and a big challenge, too.

01:07:07.550 --> 01:07:10.010
My question is more centered
on, I guess, a recent paper

01:07:10.010 --> 01:07:14.400
by Dr. [INAUDIBLE] about
the monoculture of Windows.

01:07:14.400 --> 01:07:16.670
You guys are so
popular that basically,

01:07:16.670 --> 01:07:18.830
if there's a security
problem and a lot of people

01:07:18.830 --> 01:07:21.530
have it, first, do you think
that that is a problem?

01:07:21.530 --> 01:07:25.680
And second, what kind of
solutions do you guys think

01:07:25.680 --> 01:07:27.410
should be applied to that?

01:07:27.410 --> 01:07:30.035
GATES: Well, it is not--

01:07:33.470 --> 01:07:35.500
and if you say, okay, you're--

01:07:35.500 --> 01:07:37.990
there's two types of
security things that go on.

01:07:37.990 --> 01:07:39.760
There's people who want fame.

01:07:39.760 --> 01:07:46.100
And they like to attack
the most popular system.

01:07:46.100 --> 01:07:50.320
And as we get down the
learning curve of making it

01:07:50.320 --> 01:07:53.680
so they don't
succeed, that actually

01:07:53.680 --> 01:07:55.810
deals with the most
serious security threat.

01:07:55.810 --> 01:07:57.430
The serious security
threat is not

01:07:57.430 --> 01:08:00.020
somebody who just wants fame.

01:08:00.020 --> 01:08:03.050
It's somebody who really
wants to steal information.

01:08:03.050 --> 01:08:08.500
And so as we beat the
fame-oriented IQ and drive that

01:08:08.500 --> 01:08:10.870
regularity down, we
actually create--

01:08:10.870 --> 01:08:12.790
we'll create the
first system that's

01:08:12.790 --> 01:08:16.660
truly secure that is tested
by lots and lots of IQ

01:08:16.660 --> 01:08:20.020
and come up with much
richer methodologies that

01:08:20.020 --> 01:08:21.490
allow that to work.

01:08:21.490 --> 01:08:25.000
And so there's
breakthrough ideas

01:08:25.000 --> 01:08:26.770
that come out of
research in universities

01:08:26.770 --> 01:08:30.090
that are allowing us to get to
that extremely secure point.

01:08:30.090 --> 01:08:33.790
It's a process of a number
of years looking toward.

01:08:33.790 --> 01:08:36.490
The approach where you
say, hey, let's have--

01:08:36.490 --> 01:08:38.420
how many operating
systems do you want?

01:08:38.420 --> 01:08:38.920
50?

01:08:38.920 --> 01:08:40.569
100?

01:08:40.569 --> 01:08:42.200
That just says to
a company, hey,

01:08:42.200 --> 01:08:45.151
your salary data is on a hundred
different operating systems.

01:08:45.151 --> 01:08:47.109
And applications-- people
have to try and write

01:08:47.109 --> 01:08:48.910
software for a hundred
different operating systems.

01:08:48.910 --> 01:08:49.569
Oops.

01:08:49.569 --> 01:08:51.069
Now, you can't buy
the same applications.

01:08:51.069 --> 01:08:52.152
That would be monoculture.

01:08:52.152 --> 01:08:54.735
You've got to buy a hundred
different word processors.

01:08:54.735 --> 01:08:56.109
And everybody else
learned those,

01:08:56.109 --> 01:08:57.370
but they don't change data.

01:08:57.370 --> 01:08:59.170
But what's the
security statement?

01:08:59.170 --> 01:09:01.720
All you need is one bug in
one of the hundred operating

01:09:01.720 --> 01:09:03.880
systems or one of the
hundred word processors,

01:09:03.880 --> 01:09:07.080
and your salary data
is out to the world.

01:09:07.080 --> 01:09:10.330
Variety-- just say
V equals variety.

01:09:10.330 --> 01:09:15.189
V increases the threat area.

01:09:15.189 --> 01:09:19.180
It may slow down the idea of
these spreading functions that

01:09:19.180 --> 01:09:21.040
are done for fame,
but it doesn't make

01:09:21.040 --> 01:09:23.149
information any more secure.

01:09:23.149 --> 01:09:24.340
So what does the world need?

01:09:24.340 --> 01:09:29.446
The world needs a small
number of operating systems

01:09:29.446 --> 01:09:32.890
that have firewalling
techniques, and isolation

01:09:32.890 --> 01:09:35.380
techniques, and
verification techniques that

01:09:35.380 --> 01:09:37.180
makes them secure.

01:09:37.180 --> 01:09:39.850
And in a sense, we're
on the fastest learning

01:09:39.850 --> 01:09:41.710
curve moving to that.

01:09:41.710 --> 01:09:45.229
It's the most urgent thing
we're doing and it's of our R&amp;D.

01:09:45.229 --> 01:09:48.250
That's several billion a year,
just focused on that challenge.

01:09:48.250 --> 01:09:52.540
And so variety is
not the answer.

01:09:52.540 --> 01:09:55.870
Technology and the
investments we're making

01:09:55.870 --> 01:09:57.652
are what drive
towards that answer.

01:09:57.652 --> 01:09:59.860
MODERATOR: I'm sorry to say
we have time for one more

01:09:59.860 --> 01:10:03.070
question, so please make it
one that Bill Gates has never

01:10:03.070 --> 01:10:03.700
heard before.

01:10:03.700 --> 01:10:05.029
[LAUGHTER]

01:10:06.360 --> 01:10:09.430
AUDIENCE: Wow,
that's pretty tough.

01:10:09.430 --> 01:10:11.230
I guess this is a
finance question.

01:10:11.230 --> 01:10:14.110
Microsoft currently
has around $50 billion

01:10:14.110 --> 01:10:17.589
in cash equivalents or
short-term securities,

01:10:17.589 --> 01:10:19.630
a number that's going to
reach about $100 billion

01:10:19.630 --> 01:10:20.570
in a few years.

01:10:20.570 --> 01:10:22.810
What do you see Microsoft
doing with that money?

01:10:22.810 --> 01:10:25.720
Or how do you justify
holding that much cash?

01:10:25.720 --> 01:10:27.139
[LAUGHTER]

01:10:30.892 --> 01:10:32.100
GATES: I sleep well at night.

01:10:32.100 --> 01:10:33.435
[LAUGHTER]

01:10:35.310 --> 01:10:39.200
No, your question is actually
a very serious question and one

01:10:39.200 --> 01:10:42.780
that we have.

01:10:42.780 --> 01:10:44.510
The interesting
thing about software

01:10:44.510 --> 01:10:49.190
is it's not really based on
building a lot of factories

01:10:49.190 --> 01:10:51.440
or buying a lot of materials.

01:10:51.440 --> 01:10:54.860
And going back to the
early days of Microsoft,

01:10:54.860 --> 01:10:57.480
Microsoft has been profitable.

01:10:57.480 --> 01:11:01.580
And we never had an
internet business model.

01:11:01.580 --> 01:11:04.850
It's ironic that we actually
took the company public at all,

01:11:04.850 --> 01:11:05.960
because we never--

01:11:05.960 --> 01:11:08.060
that money is still sitting
in our bank account.

01:11:08.060 --> 01:11:10.470
We never spent a
dime of that money.

01:11:10.470 --> 01:11:13.070
It was really just to
get the incentive system

01:11:13.070 --> 01:11:17.240
for the employees, which
as net has been more

01:11:17.240 --> 01:11:19.790
generous than any
incentive system

01:11:19.790 --> 01:11:22.520
that any company has ever done.

01:11:22.520 --> 01:11:26.240
And so that was a good
thing in that dimension.

01:11:26.240 --> 01:11:28.610
But it wasn't to raise capital.

01:11:28.610 --> 01:11:32.160
And so we've
accumulated capital.

01:11:32.160 --> 01:11:33.770
We've done buybacks
over the years.

01:11:33.770 --> 01:11:35.720
I think we've spent
what? $30 billion

01:11:35.720 --> 01:11:37.760
on buybacks over the years.

01:11:37.760 --> 01:11:41.139
We've instituted a dividend
that was modest at first,

01:11:41.139 --> 01:11:41.930
then we doubled it.

01:11:41.930 --> 01:11:43.580
It's still fairly modest.

01:11:43.580 --> 01:11:46.070
And as we're moving
forward, we do

01:11:46.070 --> 01:11:48.715
need to continually
evaluate this.

01:11:48.715 --> 01:11:50.840
We've tried to manage the
investments and the money

01:11:50.840 --> 01:11:51.680
very well.

01:11:51.680 --> 01:11:54.530
But there'll come a point where
we-- either through buyback,

01:11:54.530 --> 01:11:58.980
or dividend, or whatever
other approach might exist,

01:11:58.980 --> 01:12:04.190
we probably will change the
balance sheet of the company

01:12:04.190 --> 01:12:05.150
and make it more pure.

01:12:05.150 --> 01:12:08.060
Because what we're all
about is one thing--

01:12:08.060 --> 01:12:09.530
developing software.

01:12:09.530 --> 01:12:12.500
And we want-- when
people buy our stock

01:12:12.500 --> 01:12:15.010
or think about our
company, that should be it.

01:12:15.010 --> 01:12:19.950
It shouldn't be about how
we invest our treasury thing

01:12:19.950 --> 01:12:21.290
or anything else.

01:12:21.290 --> 01:12:23.240
Just about are we--

01:12:23.240 --> 01:12:24.890
is there that
opportunity in software?

01:12:24.890 --> 01:12:27.699
And are we the
company seizing it?

01:12:27.699 --> 01:12:29.948
MODERATOR: Thank you and
thank you for your questions.

01:12:29.948 --> 01:12:31.415
[APPLAUSE]

01:12:37.772 --> 01:12:39.630
VEST: Well, Bill,
thank you very much.

01:12:39.630 --> 01:12:43.840
And of course, you can't leave
MIT without your very on MIT

01:12:43.840 --> 01:12:44.878
sweatshirt.

01:12:47.630 --> 01:12:50.020
GATES: It's great.

01:12:50.020 --> 01:12:53.530
VEST: And we know that probably
every university gives you

01:12:53.530 --> 01:12:55.840
a sweatshirt, but we're
the only one that's also

01:12:55.840 --> 01:12:58.420
going to give you a hard hat.

01:12:58.420 --> 01:13:01.630
And on the front is a picture
of the William H. Gates Building

01:13:01.630 --> 01:13:03.040
taken a few minutes ago.

01:13:03.040 --> 01:13:03.670
GATES: Great.

01:13:03.670 --> 01:13:04.420
VEST: Good health.

01:13:04.420 --> 01:13:05.128
GATES: Thank you.

01:13:05.128 --> 01:13:07.150
[INAUDIBLE]

01:13:07.150 --> 01:13:08.700
Thanks.

